WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.994
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.925 --> 00:00:16.050
MODERATOR: The Massachusetts
Space Grant Consortium

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:20.070
is a NASA-funded
program whereby we're

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:23.850
trying to distribute
government funds and industry

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:27.420
funds to worthy students
and faculty members

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.400
throughout the
Commonwealth in an attempt

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.430
to keep the spirit alive
of the importance of space

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.500
exploration and
spacecraft technology

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.230
and make sure that the next
generation is able to have

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:39.410
some good fun that
those of us who

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:43.290
were in the origins of
the space program has had.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.350
The members of the Space
Consortium in this state

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.870
led by MIT and through the
Boston University, the Draper

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:52.830
Laboratory, Harvard, Tufts,
University of Massachusetts,

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.260
Wellesley College in Worcester
Polytechnic Institute,

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:58.650
newly added this year, the Woods
Hole Oceanographic Institute,

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:00.870
and starting this summer,
the Five College astronomy

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.650
department [INAUDIBLE]
in the Pioneer Valley.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:08.850
We have programs that
involve summer internships,

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.600
undergraduate research
opportunities, and fellowships.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:13.830
And those who would
like to find out more

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:17.230
can find us on the web.

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.470
The speaker this afternoon,
Christopher McKay,

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:22.980
follows in a line of
distinguished earlier speakers

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:24.910
beginning with Bill
Lenoir in 1990,

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:30.070
and most recently Bob
Stevens last year.

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:34.760
Chris's subject, prospects in
the search for life on Mars,

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.330
is an exciting one to all of us.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:39.170
I was reminded that our
department head in Aeronatuics

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.130
and Astronautics here
at MIT, Ed Crowley,

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.620
said this fall what
a wonderful summer it

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:47.690
had been because now following
the Allan Hills Satellite--

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:50.330
I'm sorry, the Allan
Hills meteorite

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:52.540
finding, the possibility
of life on Mars.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:53.790
Ed says, isn't this wonderful?

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:55.380
Now you go to cocktail
parties and everybody

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:56.255
wants to talk to you.

00:01:56.255 --> 00:01:56.980
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:58.620
So we're-- certainly
we're grateful,

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.650
not only for the [? finds ?]
but for the favorable public

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:05.910
awareness that is associated
with the exciting prospect

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:09.840
of life on other planets.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.940
Chris McKay is
uniquely qualified

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:16.050
to discuss the prospects for
us, for exploration of Mars,

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:16.550
with us.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:20.663
And I heard Chris talk about
this on previous occasions,

00:02:20.663 --> 00:02:22.330
and I assure you,
you're in for a treat.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.270
He received his PhD
in astrogeophysics

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:28.840
at the University of Colorado,
went to NASA's Ames Research

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:30.512
Center, where he
has been ever since.

00:02:30.512 --> 00:02:32.470
He's a research scientist
in the Space Sciences

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:36.050
Division, the recipient of
many prestigious awards,

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:37.810
including the
[INAUDIBLE] [? Urey ?]

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:40.210
Prize by the
Division of Planetary

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:43.060
Sciences in astronomy.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:46.120
In addition to his
interest in geophysics,

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:50.990
he has a strong interest in life
sciences, both on this planet

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:54.540
and what it tells
you about exobiology,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.410
or life outside this planet.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:03:00.280
This has led him to
work in such cold places

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.500
as Antarctica, Siberia,
and now Boston.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:03.230
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:05.720
Chris, I'm glad you made
it through the storm.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:08.260
We welcome you to the
Massachusetts Space Bank

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:09.455
Consortium.

00:03:09.455 --> 00:03:10.330
MCKAY: Thanks, Larry.

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:11.830
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:14.715
Hey.

00:03:14.715 --> 00:03:16.715
[? Thanks, Larry. ?]
[? What up? ?] As Larry was

00:03:16.715 --> 00:03:21.270
telling, I'm going to talk about
today is Mars and the prospects

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:25.280
for understanding whether
there was life on Mars.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.320
Great, thanks.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:34.170
Let me start by getting the
right end of the view graphs.

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:35.947
First, with just
a one word chart.

00:03:35.947 --> 00:03:37.530
It's only word chart
I use, I promise,

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:39.030
but since I'm from
NASA I'm required

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:40.700
to use at least one word chart.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:44.530
I want to motivate what I
do, what I'm interested in.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:47.270
This is a goals of
planetary sciences put

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:49.590
together by some
committee a while ago,

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.863
and it's the standard stuff.

00:03:51.863 --> 00:03:54.030
Determine the origin and
evolution the solar system,

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:55.822
understand the Earth,
survey the resources.

00:03:55.822 --> 00:03:57.920
But the one that I
like is one in the box,

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.855
and in some sense that's what
I take as my job description,

00:04:01.855 --> 00:04:03.980
to understand the relationship
between the chemical

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:05.870
and physical evolution of the
solar system, the appearance

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:06.270
of life.

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.228
And this is what got me
into planetary science,

00:04:08.228 --> 00:04:10.160
and this is what I really do.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:11.863
And just so you
know, just last week,

00:04:11.863 --> 00:04:13.280
when I was doing
my taxes, there's

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:14.960
a little slot this
says occupation,

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:17.418
I started writing, to understand
the relationship between--

00:04:17.418 --> 00:04:18.740
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:20.183
I've never been
audited, you know?

00:04:20.183 --> 00:04:20.970
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:22.553
I think they're
afraid of what they'll

00:04:22.553 --> 00:04:23.990
find when they come and look.

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:26.060
Well, suppose that was
your job, to understand

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:28.100
the relationship between the
chemical and physical evolution

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.892
of the solar system
and the origin of life.

00:04:29.892 --> 00:04:32.570
Obviously, most of your
work would be on Earth.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:33.650
There's good samples.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:34.775
There's a good planet here.

00:04:34.775 --> 00:04:35.870
It's easy to get to.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:36.790
A lot of lifeforms.

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:39.440
But other than the Earth, the
planet that's the next best

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:40.710
is Mars.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:44.675
And so I'm going to talk about
how Mars compares to Earth, how

00:04:44.675 --> 00:04:47.630
it represents a second
example, possibly, of life,

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:49.640
and our understanding
of it as a planet

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.650
and how we might investigate,
and in particular,

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:52.970
where we would go.

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:56.550
And I'll use places
on Earth as analogs,

00:04:56.550 --> 00:05:00.260
and our study in places like
Earth, very cold, dry places.

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:03.380
Like Larry said,
Antarctica, Siberia,

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:06.210
and now here at-- in Boston.

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:07.230
Today-- as models.

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:08.730
Let's start with
this image of Mars,

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:10.020
which is a ground-based image.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.020
And if you go out, Mars
is in the night sky now.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:13.603
You can look through
a small telescope

00:05:13.603 --> 00:05:15.860
and see the polar caps and
watch them grow and shrink

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:19.355
and dark features,
moving north and south.

00:05:19.355 --> 00:05:21.230
It's reasonable to
suspect that this would've

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:22.813
been water, and this
vegetation, and I

00:05:22.813 --> 00:05:25.040
think that's what led
to the notion of Mars

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:28.100
as the planet most likely
to have life, maybe even

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:31.850
civilizations, because of this
comparison with seasonal change

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:32.660
on Earth.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:36.800
And partly for that
reason, in '76,

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:40.160
Viking went to Mars, a little
more than 20 years ago.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.090
And its mission was
to search for life.

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:44.990
As you may remember, it had
a robotic arm to reach out,

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:47.390
scooped up some dirt, and
looked to see if there is

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:49.252
any microbial life in the soil.

00:05:49.252 --> 00:05:50.960
We knew enough by then
to know that there

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:55.430
was no widespread biosphere
on Mars, no civilization,

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:56.790
no forests, anything like that.

00:05:56.790 --> 00:06:00.590
But there's still a possibility
that there was microbial life.

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:02.220
What did Viking find?

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:04.070
I won't go into detail,
but basically, it

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:07.880
found that the soil had some
sort of reactivity, some kind

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:10.940
of oxygen, bleach or something.

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:13.500
It found that there was no
organics in the soil at all.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:14.900
So the reactivity
is-- that's why

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:17.270
the activity was thought to be
caused by some kind of chemical

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:17.810
reaction.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:19.640
So it's not very
interesting from a bio--

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:22.220
a life point of view.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:24.880
The results of Viking
really were, it's dead, Jim.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:26.180
There's nothing there.

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:27.800
Why is Mars so dead?

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:30.650
I think the answers--

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:32.282
where is the answer?

00:06:32.282 --> 00:06:33.740
Well, let's look
at the atmosphere.

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:35.840
Just to remind you what the
atmosphere of Mars is like,

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:37.215
it's not [? in ?]
the atmosphere.

00:06:37.215 --> 00:06:39.380
This is the atmosphere
you'd expect, mostly carbon

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:41.030
dioxide, nitrogen, argon.

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:42.922
Very similar to the
atmosphere of Venus,

00:06:42.922 --> 00:06:44.630
very similar to the
atmosphere that Earth

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:45.780
had, early in its history.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:47.450
In fact, this is the
kind of atmosphere

00:06:47.450 --> 00:06:48.742
you'd expect the Earth to have.

00:06:48.742 --> 00:06:50.900
It's only recently
that it's been polluted

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:54.080
by the presence of oxygen,
only a billion or two years.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:56.330
Life has been producing
this pollutant oxygen,

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:57.170
replacing the CO2.

00:06:57.170 --> 00:06:59.180
And of course, human
beings, in their wisdom,

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:00.500
are getting rid of
this polluting oxygen

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:01.685
and bringing back the CO2--

00:07:01.685 --> 00:07:02.510
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:05.510
--restoring the Earth to
its environmentally-correct

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:07.745
condition, which is
this kind of atmosphere,

00:07:07.745 --> 00:07:09.620
which is what Earth-like
planets should have.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:11.612
So the atmosphere of
Mars is not surprising,

00:07:11.612 --> 00:07:13.820
and you can see that all
the elements needed for life

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:14.330
are here.

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:16.400
CO2, nitrogen, water.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.940
And in fact, as we talk later
about humans going to Mars,

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:22.415
imagine taking this CO2
and taking the C off.

00:07:22.415 --> 00:07:23.915
You have oxygen,
nitrogen, and those

00:07:23.915 --> 00:07:26.750
are the components of breathable
air, and here's water.

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:29.390
Basic requirements needed
to sustain humans on Mars

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:33.680
are right there in the
atmosphere, ready to get to.

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:36.620
So it's not the atmosphere, per
se, that's surprising on Mars.

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:39.290
What's surprising is how
thin that atmosphere is.

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:42.200
And let me just show you the
Viking results for three Mars

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:43.910
years, six Earth years.

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:46.370
This is a pressure at
the two Viking sites,

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:48.010
slightly different elevation.

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:49.820
And the pressure's in
units of millibars.

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:52.490
As you know on Earth, sea
level pressure is a thousand

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:53.640
in these units.

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:56.285
So the pressure on Mars
is a hundred times thinner

00:07:56.285 --> 00:07:57.410
than the pressure on Earth.

00:07:57.410 --> 00:08:00.350
It's equivalent to the pressure
about 20 miles high on Earth.

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:01.890
This is important
in two respects.

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:04.550
First, for humans,
the vapor pressure

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:08.060
of water at body temperature,
30 centigrade skin temperature,

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:10.430
is about 60
millibars, which means

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:14.300
that fluids exposed to Mars
environment at body temperature

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:16.140
would start boiling.

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:18.550
It's not recommended, which
is why if you go to Mars,

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:19.550
we'll need spacesuits.

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:21.550
From a point of view of
humans, it might as well

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:23.010
be the vacuum of space.

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:24.680
Also, the triple point
pressure of water

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:26.653
is here at 6 millibars.

00:08:26.653 --> 00:08:28.070
So it says that
water is not going

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:30.890
to exist very readily
as a liquid on Mars.

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:33.120
On Mars, water is going
to behave the way carbon

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:34.720
dioxide does on Earth.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:37.059
As you know, dry ice, if
you take a chunk of dry ice

00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:39.559
and warm it up, it goes directly
from the solid to the vapor

00:08:39.559 --> 00:08:42.110
without forming a liquid
phase, which is why it's called

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:43.130
dry ice.

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:44.720
On Mars, the pressure
is low enough

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.700
that water does the same thing.

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:48.230
And then fundamentally,
I think that

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:51.320
is the answer to the question--
or to the double question,

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:54.350
why is Mars so dead and red?

00:08:54.350 --> 00:08:55.460
I think it's related.

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:58.040
And the question really is
that at the present epoch,

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:00.690
there is no liquid water
at any place, at any time.

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:03.290
This is a profound observation,
because if we look at life

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:05.870
on Earth, life on Earth
is really, essentially,

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:07.560
bags of liquid water.

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:09.540
That's what all life
forms on Earth are.

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:10.970
There's some
dormant stages where

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:13.100
they can live without
water, but all life

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:14.850
in its growing,
reproductive phases,

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:16.520
is bags of liquid water.

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:19.100
No liquid water, it's not
surprising that it's dead.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:20.990
Doesn't mean there's
no water on Mars.

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:23.210
Here's my favorite picture
from Viking, showing

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:24.530
the surface of the planet.

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:26.030
See these white patches?

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:27.410
This is water frost.

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:29.810
But as I said earlier,
as that ground warms up,

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:31.490
it's going to go
directly into vapor.

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:33.360
There's no point
is there liquid.

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:35.100
Well, if that was
the whole story

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:37.308
in terms of biology on Mars,
it would be pretty dull.

00:09:37.308 --> 00:09:39.142
But one of the things
that Viking did find--

00:09:39.142 --> 00:09:41.210
I'm sure you're all
familiar with these images

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:43.970
showing fluvial features
on Mars, evidence

00:09:43.970 --> 00:09:46.120
that at one time, Mars
had lots of water.

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:49.700
This-- the scale on this feature
is some 300 kilometers across,

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:52.790
showing fluvial features carving
through the ancient crater

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:53.820
terrain.

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:54.850
You can see the heavy--

00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:56.930
heavily the evidence
of craters indicating

00:09:56.930 --> 00:09:59.690
that this terrain dates back
to the late bombardment,

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:02.220
some 3.8 billion years ago.

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:04.002
This is, essentially, the--

00:10:04.002 --> 00:10:05.960
I think these are the
most interesting pictures

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:08.090
we have of anything
beyond the Earth,

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:09.767
from the point of view of life.

00:10:09.767 --> 00:10:11.600
We don't have any
evidence directly of life.

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:13.830
We have the next best thing,
which is liquid water.

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:15.455
We don't even have
liquid water, but we

00:10:15.455 --> 00:10:17.400
have pictures of what
used to be liquid water.

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:19.550
So we're several steps
away, but still, these

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:21.620
are the most exciting
pictures that we

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:25.137
have from a biological point of
view once we leave the Earth.

00:10:25.137 --> 00:10:26.720
Now, just as a word
of caution, here's

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:28.610
another interesting
feature, picture,

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:31.250
that looks like a fluvial
feature, nice [? oxbows ?]

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:31.780
and whatnot.

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:34.100
But this is Venus,
and the temperatures

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:37.138
were some 600 degrees
when this fluid flowed.

00:10:37.138 --> 00:10:38.180
So one has to be careful.

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:40.550
How do we really know that
these features are water?

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:44.420
I won't go into a lot of
detail, but the morphology

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:45.570
of the feature.

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:48.330
This is down flow this way, so
it's a tributary system, not

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:50.300
a distributary system,
so it's unlikely

00:10:50.300 --> 00:10:52.870
that it's anything but
a distributed fluid.

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:55.190
Candidates, CO2,
water, whatever.

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:57.020
Water is the logical one.

00:10:57.020 --> 00:10:59.310
It's the only explanation
that really holds water,

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:00.870
so to speak.

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:04.040
So we'll take this as
evidence that at one time,

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:05.840
there was lots of water on Mars.

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:07.310
We have other
indications that Mars

00:11:07.310 --> 00:11:12.380
was more active in the past,
for example, Olympus Mons.

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:15.030
This is volcanoes,
large volcanoes on Mars.

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:18.080
This one's 27 kilometers
high, 500 kilometers across.

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:20.720
All indications
are that Mars was

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:22.500
an active world at one time.

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:24.810
We have not seen any
eruptions at the present time.

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:28.490
We have no direct evidence
of current volcanism on Mars,

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:31.790
and there's a lot of
evidence of past volcanism.

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:33.050
So it's interesting.

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:35.960
Everything we know about Mars
from the images from Viking

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:37.910
would tend to indicate
that it was at one time

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:41.240
an active planet and
has become inactive.

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:43.490
What was it like
when it was active?

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:45.200
Well, I put up this
picture to bring

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:48.650
to mind the notion that early in
Mars history when it had water,

00:11:48.650 --> 00:11:50.380
it might have looked like this.

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:52.100
Now, this picture
is actually drawn

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:55.820
based on taking the estimates
of the amount of water required

00:11:55.820 --> 00:11:57.195
to carve the channels.

00:11:57.195 --> 00:11:58.070
Look at the channels.

00:11:58.070 --> 00:11:59.445
We know the size
of the channels.

00:11:59.445 --> 00:12:00.920
Calculate how much
water that is.

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:02.990
Extrapolate to the planet.

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:06.590
We deduce a layer of water from
that geomorphological argument

00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:08.210
of 500 meters thick.

00:12:08.210 --> 00:12:11.660
If you take 500 meters of water
spread over the whole globe,

00:12:11.660 --> 00:12:13.880
sort of pour it onto
the present topography,

00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:15.500
sort of like that
Sherwin-Williams ad

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:17.390
where they're pouring
paint over the globe,

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:19.730
just let it relax into
the present topography.

00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:21.530
This is the correct
image that one gets.

00:12:21.530 --> 00:12:23.120
The northern
lowlands are flooded.

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:25.850
Valles Marineris is flooded,
and then the southern highlands

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:28.020
[? or ?] [? Tarsus ?]
is above ground.

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:29.370
This is a realistic picture.

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:30.425
This is a picture--

00:12:30.425 --> 00:12:33.600
it's an artist conception
of what Mars was like 3

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:35.732
and 1/2 billion years
ago, might have been like.

00:12:35.732 --> 00:12:37.190
Sometimes I say,
well, this picture

00:12:37.190 --> 00:12:39.620
is taken by Hubble, because it
can look backwards in time--

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:39.800
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:41.792
--and this is a photograph
of Mars, 3 and 1/2--

00:12:41.792 --> 00:12:43.250
but you guys know
better than that,

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:46.152
so I won't even try to
convince you of that.

00:12:46.152 --> 00:12:47.610
Now, when you look
at this picture,

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:50.700
I'm sure it must remind you
of the Earth seen from space.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:53.240
The famous-- what's become
essentially a visual icon

00:12:53.240 --> 00:12:56.120
for the space age, the picture
of the Blue Marble Earth

00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:58.610
seen from space,
Apollo 17 picture.

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:02.350
And that's really the essential
point in the whole discussion

00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:04.350
of Mars and what motivates
the interest of Mars,

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:07.460
that at one time, there might
have been two blue marbles,

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:08.780
Earth and Mars.

00:13:08.780 --> 00:13:09.900
That's very interesting.

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:12.025
It's also interesting that
somewhere along the line

00:13:12.025 --> 00:13:15.690
we lost one of our marbles, and
something went wrong with it.

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:17.700
But to me, the most
interesting thing

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:19.660
is not that there was
another Earth, per se,

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:22.570
but that Earth and Mars
were similar at a time.

00:13:22.570 --> 00:13:25.120
When early Earth
and early Mars were

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:27.880
similar at a time when
early Earth had life.

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:29.050
We look at early Earth.

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:32.830
We know that it had water,
CO2, atmosphere, volcanism.

00:13:32.830 --> 00:13:36.250
We know that early Mars
had water, CO2, atmosphere,

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:37.080
volcanism.

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:38.920
But we know that the
time they were similar

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:41.513
was a time when we first see
evidence for life on Earth.

00:13:41.513 --> 00:13:43.930
And let me go into this in a
little more detail, because I

00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:47.140
think it's a very fascinating
story, the early history

00:13:47.140 --> 00:13:48.640
of the planets.

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:51.820
Here is the history of the
Earth and Mars compared.

00:13:51.820 --> 00:13:53.740
Let's start with
the Earth forming

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:56.770
4 and 1/2 billion years ago.

00:13:56.770 --> 00:13:59.050
The moon forming event
somewhere around here,

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:02.080
for [? 4.6 ?] or something, but
the Earth is really forming,

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:03.280
the surface is stabilizing.

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:06.500
The end of the late bombardment,
3.8 billion years ago.

00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:09.700
So I take that as a birthday
of the Earth, April 1st,

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:11.000
3.8 billion years ago.

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:11.500
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:14.020
That's when the Earth
yesterday-- was its birthday.

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:15.290
3.8 billion years ago.

00:14:15.290 --> 00:14:17.650
And what's interesting from
a point of view of biology

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:19.120
is that we have
evidence for life

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:24.310
at 3.8, 3.9 in that form
of chemical fossils.

00:14:24.310 --> 00:14:27.250
Life selectively takes
carbon-12 over carbon-13

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:29.230
by about a few percent, 2%.

00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:31.150
And we see that
isotopic signature

00:14:31.150 --> 00:14:32.920
in sedimentary record.

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:35.490
It's not really hard evidence.

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:37.640
It probably wouldn't stand
up in a criminal trial,

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:39.610
but you might get by
with in a civil trial--

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:40.110
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:40.110 --> 00:14:44.290
--in terms of evidence
for life, okay?

00:14:44.290 --> 00:14:46.690
But-- and that's why I
call it possible life here.

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:48.400
And it's virtually
instantaneous,

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:50.630
if not before the end of
the [? end ?] [? late ?]

00:14:50.630 --> 00:14:52.090
bombardment.

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:55.570
Where 3.5, we have
definite evidence.

00:14:55.570 --> 00:14:57.580
This is beyond a
shadow of a doubt,

00:14:57.580 --> 00:15:00.580
beyond reasonable doubt,
direct evidence for life at 3.5

00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:01.880
in terms of fossil algae.

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:04.750
And I'll show you those images
from those kind of rocks.

00:15:04.750 --> 00:15:09.370
So this is amazing in that
it shows that Earth forms,

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:12.970
and then essentially, possibly
instantly, geologically

00:15:12.970 --> 00:15:15.340
speaking, where an instant
is 10 million years,

00:15:15.340 --> 00:15:17.440
life appears on the planet.

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:19.420
We can't reproduce this
in the lab, by the way,

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:21.845
and we don't have a consensus
theory for how it happened.

00:15:21.845 --> 00:15:24.220
We just have the evidence that
it happened really quickly

00:15:24.220 --> 00:15:24.957
on Earth.

00:15:24.957 --> 00:15:27.040
And then what happens,
just to complete the story,

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:29.900
as it's pretty dull for a long
time, we have microbial life,

00:15:29.900 --> 00:15:34.340
and then eventually oxygen
builds up, multicellular life,

00:15:34.340 --> 00:15:36.700
and then very recently,
animals, and of course--

00:15:36.700 --> 00:15:39.160
just to keep perspective, here
is the dinosaur extinctions,

00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:40.010
just there.

00:15:40.010 --> 00:15:43.685
And I tried to fit in some
more events like the election

00:15:43.685 --> 00:15:46.060
last year in there, but it
just doesn't fit in the scale.

00:15:46.060 --> 00:15:49.270
So it's really-- this
first half billion years

00:15:49.270 --> 00:15:51.250
that's the time period
that's of interest

00:15:51.250 --> 00:15:53.200
in terms of life on planets.

00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:56.050
And in fact, our current
understanding of the biology

00:15:56.050 --> 00:15:58.840
would suggest that
by 3.5 for sure,

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:01.510
all major metabolic
pathways were in place.

00:16:01.510 --> 00:16:05.650
We had DNA life, DNA-based
life like we have today,

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:08.620
with only two major
pathways not yet realized,

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:11.780
and that is aerobic respiration,
which we all depend on,

00:16:11.780 --> 00:16:15.260
and the ability to metabolize
silicon parts like diatoms do.

00:16:15.260 --> 00:16:18.578
Other than that, the
story is written by 3.5.

00:16:18.578 --> 00:16:20.620
I call these years, the
first half billion years,

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:21.578
the Wonder Bread years.

00:16:21.578 --> 00:16:23.953
These are the years-- you
remember those old commercials?

00:16:23.953 --> 00:16:26.830
Certainly dates in terms of
what I watched on TV, but it's--

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:28.690
this were the years,
the formative years,

00:16:28.690 --> 00:16:31.850
when Earth and life are learning
to work and live together.

00:16:31.850 --> 00:16:36.190
By 3.5, really, it's
all details after that.

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:37.910
Well, let's go to Mars.

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:39.370
This is the same period, 4--

00:16:39.370 --> 00:16:41.778
3.5 to 4, when we think
there was water on Mars.

00:16:41.778 --> 00:16:42.820
And why do we think that?

00:16:42.820 --> 00:16:45.640
It's because the fluvial
channels interlace

00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:47.350
the ancient crater terrain.

00:16:47.350 --> 00:16:50.500
It's very convenient that the
surfaces in the solar system

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:51.910
are time-stamped.

00:16:51.910 --> 00:16:54.940
There was a big giant time
stamping a 3.8 billion years

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:56.905
ago called the ancient crater--

00:16:56.905 --> 00:16:58.280
the end of the
heavy bombardment.

00:16:58.280 --> 00:16:59.950
We look at the lunar highlands.

00:16:59.950 --> 00:17:02.530
We're seeing material--
we're seeing that timestamp.

00:17:02.530 --> 00:17:04.480
We're seeing a surface
that dates back

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:05.589
to 3.8 billion years.

00:17:05.589 --> 00:17:08.256
When we look at Mercury, when we
look at the ancient crater tray

00:17:08.256 --> 00:17:10.150
on Mars, we see that timestamp.

00:17:10.150 --> 00:17:11.740
On Earth, that
timestamp has been

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:15.760
erased by the very activity
that's kept Earth alive,

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:17.470
but we see it on
Mars, and we see

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:20.290
that [? while ?] fluvial
features predominantly

00:17:20.290 --> 00:17:22.069
are associated in
that ancient terrain.

00:17:22.069 --> 00:17:25.650
So that tells us that water
flowed during this point,

00:17:25.650 --> 00:17:27.310
some time before and after.

00:17:27.310 --> 00:17:30.220
And that's the time when
life is found on Earth.

00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:32.920
That is, I think, a
very compelling argument

00:17:32.920 --> 00:17:35.710
that if Earth and Mars
were indeed similar,

00:17:35.710 --> 00:17:37.980
and similarity really
means only liquid water.

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:40.480
From a biological point of view,
the only thing that matters

00:17:40.480 --> 00:17:41.950
is liquid water.

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:44.260
And I'll show you
arguments for that.

00:17:44.260 --> 00:17:45.437
Then-- and Earth--

00:17:45.437 --> 00:17:47.270
life appears quickly
on Earth, then could it

00:17:47.270 --> 00:17:48.680
have appeared on Mars?

00:17:48.680 --> 00:17:52.510
That's the essential
scientific question.

00:17:52.510 --> 00:17:55.370
Let me answer a
related question,

00:17:55.370 --> 00:17:58.000
which is Earth and Mars
started off so similar.

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:00.250
Why did they take such
different trajectories?

00:18:00.250 --> 00:18:02.830
Why does-- why did Mars go bad?

00:18:02.830 --> 00:18:04.260
Why do planets go bad?

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:08.050
Is it bad schools or
bad neighborhoods?

00:18:08.050 --> 00:18:10.000
Federal programs?

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:11.692
What's the problem?

00:18:11.692 --> 00:18:13.150
We have some
understanding of that.

00:18:13.150 --> 00:18:15.820
We have some theories
for why Earth stayed good

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:18.220
and why Mars didn't,
and they have

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:19.900
to do with the carbon cycle.

00:18:19.900 --> 00:18:23.140
Imagine the early Mars,
early Earth as well,

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:24.820
with thick CO2 atmospheres.

00:18:24.820 --> 00:18:27.490
These atmospheres are required
to keep the planets warm.

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:29.860
Now, interestingly,
we can't consistently

00:18:29.860 --> 00:18:32.800
explain Mars' early climate
with a CO2 atmosphere,

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:35.830
but nonetheless, let's take
it that Mars had a thick CO2

00:18:35.830 --> 00:18:36.730
atmosphere.

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:39.670
The CO2 is not stable in the
presence of water, represented

00:18:39.670 --> 00:18:41.140
here by these clouds.

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:44.020
The water will dissolve the
CO2 to form a weak acid, which

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:46.250
will weather silicate
rocks, and that result

00:18:46.250 --> 00:18:48.560
will be the formation
of calcium carbonate.

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:50.650
Now, on Earth,
organisms have made

00:18:50.650 --> 00:18:54.130
use of this saturation
of calcium carbonate

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:55.520
to make shells and other things.

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:58.510
But even the absence
of life, if left alone,

00:18:58.510 --> 00:19:01.480
the CO2 would turn into solid
material on ocean and lake

00:19:01.480 --> 00:19:04.060
basin floors, chalk
and limestone deposits

00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:05.710
on the bottom of
ocean and lakes.

00:19:05.710 --> 00:19:07.990
Well, on Earth,
fortunately, that material

00:19:07.990 --> 00:19:10.000
is carried by
subduction to depths

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:12.880
where temperatures of around
1,100 degrees centigrade,

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:15.130
the carbonate turns
back to CO2 and comes

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:16.937
bubbling up in arc volcanoes.

00:19:16.937 --> 00:19:18.520
This is exactly
what's happening, say,

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:20.515
at Cascade Range.

00:19:20.515 --> 00:19:23.680
The Juan de Fuca oceanic plate
is subducting under the North

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:25.120
American continental plate.

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:27.730
Those sediments volatilize,
and they come bubbling up,

00:19:27.730 --> 00:19:30.820
and that's the gases that are
emanating from the Cascade

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:31.510
Range.

00:19:31.510 --> 00:19:34.390
So the gases coming
out of Mount St. Helens

00:19:34.390 --> 00:19:37.120
are literally the
recycling of carbon

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:39.280
that was deposited
in the Pacific Ocean.

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:42.940
So while those gases and
ashes were an inconvenience

00:19:42.940 --> 00:19:44.823
to the people living
near Mount St. Helens,

00:19:44.823 --> 00:19:46.490
in terms of the health
of the biosphere,

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:48.680
it's an important
recycling mechanism.

00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:52.235
Well, on Mars, there there's
no evidence that this occurs.

00:19:52.235 --> 00:19:53.860
There's no evidence
of plate tectonics.

00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:57.100
We don't see any rifting, the
spreading centers, I mean.

00:19:57.100 --> 00:19:59.110
We don't see any
subduction areas

00:19:59.110 --> 00:20:01.900
and we don't have anything
that looks like arc volcanism.

00:20:01.900 --> 00:20:03.983
So Mars being a
one-plate planet,

00:20:03.983 --> 00:20:05.650
there's no way to
recycle the carbonate.

00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:07.305
So in some simple
sense, we think

00:20:07.305 --> 00:20:08.680
what happened is
Mars started off

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:10.000
with a thick CO2 atmosphere.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:11.640
It formed carbonate deposits.

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:14.110
And there's no way to recycle,
and that was all she wrote.

00:20:14.110 --> 00:20:15.818
A hundred million
years later, everything

00:20:15.818 --> 00:20:17.800
got cold and everybody died.

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:21.310
So in some simple
sense, we think

00:20:21.310 --> 00:20:24.820
this explains the differences
between the two planets.

00:20:24.820 --> 00:20:26.590
That early period
of Mars' history,

00:20:26.590 --> 00:20:30.755
this period of half a
billion years between 4 and 3

00:20:30.755 --> 00:20:33.130
and 1/2 billion, that's the
period that we're interested.

00:20:33.130 --> 00:20:35.530
That's when Viking should
have landed on Mars.

00:20:35.530 --> 00:20:38.050
Viking was 3 and 1/2
billion years late.

00:20:38.050 --> 00:20:40.810
It wasn't NASA's fault, but
we get the blame anyway.

00:20:40.810 --> 00:20:42.220
3 and 1/2 billion years late.

00:20:42.220 --> 00:20:44.080
We have other
evidence that suggests

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:46.882
that Mars was very different
early in its history as well,

00:20:46.882 --> 00:20:48.340
and this is the
meteorite evidence.

00:20:48.340 --> 00:20:50.590
I want to talk briefly about it.

00:20:50.590 --> 00:20:52.720
We have meteorites on
Earth that came from Mars.

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:54.040
How did they get here?

00:20:54.040 --> 00:20:55.750
Well, it's represented
in this picture.

00:20:55.750 --> 00:20:58.865
What we think happens is
stuff slams into the planets.

00:20:58.865 --> 00:21:00.490
A good example is
look at the night sky

00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:02.278
and there's comet
Hale-Bopp, racing

00:21:02.278 --> 00:21:03.320
through the solar system.

00:21:03.320 --> 00:21:05.070
Fortunately, it's
missing all the planets.

00:21:05.070 --> 00:21:07.117
It's missing Earth
in particular.

00:21:07.117 --> 00:21:09.700
But if it wasn't missing them--
there's always a finite chance

00:21:09.700 --> 00:21:10.480
it would hit--

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:14.620
it could slam with enough
force to knock material in--

00:21:14.620 --> 00:21:16.673
with velocities exceeding
escape velocity,

00:21:16.673 --> 00:21:18.340
and the stuff would
never fall back down

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:20.080
onto the planet that was hit.

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:22.690
And it could go into a
solar orbit, and millions

00:21:22.690 --> 00:21:25.720
of orbits later, by
some random chance,

00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:29.030
it could hit another planet come
slamming into the atmosphere.

00:21:29.030 --> 00:21:30.010
And it would be found.

00:21:30.010 --> 00:21:31.840
In this particular case,
we find them in Antarctica.

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:33.340
Well, it's not the
meteorites have

00:21:33.340 --> 00:21:35.460
some sort of perverse
attraction for Antarctica

00:21:35.460 --> 00:21:38.370
the way tornadoes do for trailer
parks or anything like that.

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:38.870
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:40.330
It's just that in
Antarctica, it's

00:21:40.330 --> 00:21:41.950
very easy to
recognize a meteorite

00:21:41.950 --> 00:21:45.843
because a big white sheet,
and the only way a black rock

00:21:45.843 --> 00:21:47.260
can get on that
sheet is literally

00:21:47.260 --> 00:21:49.800
falling down from the sky.

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:52.390
Well, so we have
on Earth 12 rocks,

00:21:52.390 --> 00:21:55.460
a dozen rocks, that we
think came from Mars.

00:21:55.460 --> 00:21:58.270
This is a very nice
diagram by Jim Gooding

00:21:58.270 --> 00:22:02.900
that shows how these rocks
form four classes of rocks.

00:22:02.900 --> 00:22:05.800
There's the S, N, and C rocks.

00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:09.340
11 of the 12 rocks fall
into these three classes.

00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:11.680
And what I'm plotting here--
what Jim plotted here is

00:22:11.680 --> 00:22:12.590
the age--

00:22:12.590 --> 00:22:15.820
the crystallization age of the
rock, when the rock formed,

00:22:15.820 --> 00:22:19.390
versus a estimate of the
depth below the surface

00:22:19.390 --> 00:22:20.962
at which it formed.

00:22:20.962 --> 00:22:22.420
So you can see that
the S, N, and C

00:22:22.420 --> 00:22:25.810
types, which is 11 of the
12, are all fairly young,

00:22:25.810 --> 00:22:28.780
200 million years for the S type
and only about a billion years

00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:29.740
for the N and C type.

00:22:29.740 --> 00:22:31.160
These are young rocks.

00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:34.360
They formed recently on Mars,
and were then kicked into orbit

00:22:34.360 --> 00:22:35.320
and came to Earth.

00:22:35.320 --> 00:22:38.140
And then there's one
rock, a famous rock now,

00:22:38.140 --> 00:22:40.720
that was found in Antarctica,
that formed 4 and 1/2

00:22:40.720 --> 00:22:42.330
billion years ago on Mars.

00:22:42.330 --> 00:22:45.850
Well, these 11 are all
consistent with this planet

00:22:45.850 --> 00:22:49.990
that's cold, with oxidizing
water, and no organics stable.

00:22:49.990 --> 00:22:52.713
These are all consistent
with the Mars we see today,

00:22:52.713 --> 00:22:54.130
and we're telling
us that it looks

00:22:54.130 --> 00:22:56.320
like Mars has been the way
it is today for at least

00:22:56.320 --> 00:22:59.650
a billion years, a cold,
dry desert world, probably

00:22:59.650 --> 00:23:01.600
lifeless on the
surface, at least.

00:23:01.600 --> 00:23:04.930
But this one is not consistent
with that kind of environment,

00:23:04.930 --> 00:23:07.240
and it's consistent with a
warm, reducing conditions

00:23:07.240 --> 00:23:10.390
with organic compounds,
stable, again indicating

00:23:10.390 --> 00:23:12.650
Mars went through
some very deep change

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:16.070
in its environmental conditions
from an environment that was

00:23:16.070 --> 00:23:17.658
much more conducive to life.

00:23:17.658 --> 00:23:19.700
[? When ?] [? is ?]
[? it? ?] Let me just do sort

00:23:19.700 --> 00:23:24.020
of a parenthetical discussion
here about how do we know these

00:23:24.020 --> 00:23:25.100
rocks came from Mars?

00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:28.130
Because it's often
an interesting point.

00:23:28.130 --> 00:23:32.420
And I'll just go over the
evidence briefly on that.

00:23:32.420 --> 00:23:35.670
This is-- the logic
takes two steps.

00:23:35.670 --> 00:23:37.550
One is that all these
rocks, 12 rocks,

00:23:37.550 --> 00:23:38.660
came from the same place.

00:23:38.660 --> 00:23:39.680
And that's based on--

00:23:39.680 --> 00:23:43.070
primarily on oxygen isotopes,
and I just illustrate that here

00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:45.710
with a diagram that
shows the oxygen isotopes

00:23:45.710 --> 00:23:47.090
of different meteorites.

00:23:47.090 --> 00:23:48.800
And they cluster
in certain classes

00:23:48.800 --> 00:23:51.215
and the SNCs form a
distinct class right there,

00:23:51.215 --> 00:23:52.280
as you can see.

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:56.500
This is oxygen-17 to
16, oxygen-18 to 16,

00:23:56.500 --> 00:23:58.740
and ocean water would
be 0, 0 on this plot,

00:23:58.740 --> 00:24:00.770
which would be right
around about there.

00:24:00.770 --> 00:24:02.058
So you could see these form--

00:24:02.058 --> 00:24:04.350
each different meteorites
tend to form a distinct class

00:24:04.350 --> 00:24:05.240
or line on this plot.

00:24:05.240 --> 00:24:07.355
And there's a distinct
cluster there, the SNC,

00:24:07.355 --> 00:24:10.460
that tells us that these all
came from the same parent body.

00:24:10.460 --> 00:24:11.877
They all came from
the same place.

00:24:11.877 --> 00:24:14.418
[? This ?] [? doesn't ?] told
us that they've come from Mars.

00:24:14.418 --> 00:24:16.470
It just tells us they
come from the same place.

00:24:16.470 --> 00:24:17.960
But what's interesting,
and the way

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:19.940
we figure out that
they came from Mars

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:22.610
is one of these types,
and particularly this one,

00:24:22.610 --> 00:24:26.180
the Elephant Hills
79001 was analyzed

00:24:26.180 --> 00:24:28.990
by a variety of people,
including Bob [? Pepin ?]

00:24:28.990 --> 00:24:30.020
in Minnesota.

00:24:30.020 --> 00:24:34.250
And they found in that meteorite
bubbles of air, bubbles of gas.

00:24:34.250 --> 00:24:36.560
And those bubbles of
gas are exactly the same

00:24:36.560 --> 00:24:40.130
as the gas on Mars as
analyzed by Viking.

00:24:40.130 --> 00:24:42.230
This is the Mars
air in a log plot,

00:24:42.230 --> 00:24:45.350
and this is the gas in
the S-type meteorite.

00:24:45.350 --> 00:24:48.320
And the identity function
would be the solid line.

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:50.155
And here are the
points, and the size

00:24:50.155 --> 00:24:51.530
and the circles
of the error bar.

00:24:51.530 --> 00:24:54.620
So you can see that over a range
of nine orders of magnitude,

00:24:54.620 --> 00:24:56.450
the gas in this
bubble is exactly

00:24:56.450 --> 00:24:59.220
like the gas in this
meteorite on Mars,

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:01.110
which formed 200
million years ago.

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:03.620
And this is not like
the gas anywhere else.

00:25:03.620 --> 00:25:04.520
It couldn't be.

00:25:04.520 --> 00:25:06.810
This is not Earth air,
Venus air, Pluto air,

00:25:06.810 --> 00:25:07.670
or anything else.

00:25:07.670 --> 00:25:10.340
This is definitely a signature
that these things came

00:25:10.340 --> 00:25:13.130
from Mars, and particularly
the argon-36 to 40

00:25:13.130 --> 00:25:15.610
ratio is different
than anywhere else.

00:25:15.610 --> 00:25:18.240
And it would've been different
earlier in Mars' history.

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:19.710
So what it tells
us is two things.

00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:21.752
One is that Mars' atmosphere
hasn't changed much,

00:25:21.752 --> 00:25:24.590
at least over 200 million
years, and that this rock came

00:25:24.590 --> 00:25:27.410
from Mars, and therefore all
12 of them came from Mars.

00:25:27.410 --> 00:25:32.240
So that's the basic logic of why
we think they came from Mars.

00:25:32.240 --> 00:25:36.290
Now, of course, the famous
one is this Allen Hills 85001,

00:25:36.290 --> 00:25:38.360
in which interesting
features were

00:25:38.360 --> 00:25:43.790
found that look like that,
little, tiny, nondescript

00:25:43.790 --> 00:25:44.840
ovals.

00:25:44.840 --> 00:25:46.730
And the possibility
was raised that these

00:25:46.730 --> 00:25:48.570
are direct evidence of fossils.

00:25:48.570 --> 00:25:50.120
Now, I want to
make a distinction

00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:52.130
between the
geochemical evidence we

00:25:52.130 --> 00:25:55.220
had from the meteorites,
this meteorite in particular,

00:25:55.220 --> 00:25:57.740
that Mars was a
warmer, wetter place,

00:25:57.740 --> 00:26:00.620
and then this evidence that
there might have been fossils.

00:26:00.620 --> 00:26:02.990
This evidence is, I think, weak.

00:26:02.990 --> 00:26:05.280
It certainly can't be
proven wrong readily,

00:26:05.280 --> 00:26:08.755
but it's not very convincing
evidence of past life.

00:26:08.755 --> 00:26:10.130
But the evidence
of the meteorite

00:26:10.130 --> 00:26:13.620
that it was warm and
wet is much more robust.

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:15.620
So they're not
necessarily the same.

00:26:15.620 --> 00:26:16.790
Why is this evidence weak?

00:26:16.790 --> 00:26:18.680
I think partly
because these fossils

00:26:18.680 --> 00:26:21.690
are so ambiguous in terms
of evidence for life.

00:26:21.690 --> 00:26:22.970
They're very small.

00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:25.370
Typical scale is about
10 nanometers across,

00:26:25.370 --> 00:26:28.370
which is very small compared
to microbial life on Earth.

00:26:28.370 --> 00:26:31.303
And they're just nondescript,
little oval shapes.

00:26:31.303 --> 00:26:32.720
Let's compare that
to the evidence

00:26:32.720 --> 00:26:34.800
we have on Earth for fossils.

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:38.480
The earliest, oldest evidence
for life on Earth is--

00:26:38.480 --> 00:26:40.790
this is a picture of that.

00:26:40.790 --> 00:26:43.710
This is rocks that are 3
and 1/2 billion years old.

00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:47.090
This is the oldest evidence we
have for life on this planet.

00:26:47.090 --> 00:26:48.298
And these are from Australia.

00:26:48.298 --> 00:26:50.465
This is from work of
[? Bill ?] [? Scharf ?] and his

00:26:50.465 --> 00:26:51.170
colleagues.

00:26:51.170 --> 00:26:52.300
And this is a rock.

00:26:52.300 --> 00:26:53.930
It's actually picture of a rock.

00:26:53.930 --> 00:26:56.570
This shows sort of a
lasagna-like shape feature.

00:26:56.570 --> 00:26:58.880
What it is a microbial
mat, organisms living

00:26:58.880 --> 00:27:02.330
in a mat on a shoreline
or an ancient lake,

00:27:02.330 --> 00:27:06.710
and then successive waves
or years, mat builds up,

00:27:06.710 --> 00:27:08.180
and it forms a
layered structure,

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:11.060
and then it's
solidified and lithified

00:27:11.060 --> 00:27:14.230
and it forms this characteristic
shape, stromatolitic shape.

00:27:14.230 --> 00:27:17.690
And within these structures,
we can find micro fossils.

00:27:17.690 --> 00:27:20.120
This is a direct
picture A and panel A,

00:27:20.120 --> 00:27:22.460
and this is a line
representation of it.

00:27:22.460 --> 00:27:24.890
And we can see, we can
lay down a modern filament

00:27:24.890 --> 00:27:26.960
of cyanobacteria,
blue green algae,

00:27:26.960 --> 00:27:29.930
and show that
they're very similar.

00:27:29.930 --> 00:27:34.310
This is very good evidence
for biological processes,

00:27:34.310 --> 00:27:35.810
and in fact for photosynthesis.

00:27:35.810 --> 00:27:37.760
Not just for biology,
but for photosynthesis,

00:27:37.760 --> 00:27:40.590
for complete microbial
mat communities on Earth

00:27:40.590 --> 00:27:42.400
at 3.5 billion years ago.

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:44.600
And we can even make
a plausible argument

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:47.780
that these are
cyanobacterial mats.

00:27:47.780 --> 00:27:51.270
So it's much better argument
than we have for life on Mars.

00:27:51.270 --> 00:27:54.260
So the one question I
want to now turn to is,

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:56.760
if we want to go to Mars, if
we want to get better evidence,

00:27:56.760 --> 00:27:57.870
how do we do it?

00:27:57.870 --> 00:27:58.868
How would we do it?

00:27:58.868 --> 00:28:00.243
Well, I think the
answer is we're

00:28:00.243 --> 00:28:02.420
going to have to go to Mars.

00:28:02.420 --> 00:28:04.160
It's sort of a
standard sales pitch.

00:28:04.160 --> 00:28:06.440
The first rock is free.

00:28:06.440 --> 00:28:08.450
The second one
costs $500 million.

00:28:08.450 --> 00:28:09.050
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:09.050 --> 00:28:11.420
So how-- where are we
going to go on Mars

00:28:11.420 --> 00:28:13.410
to buy this $500 million rock?

00:28:13.410 --> 00:28:15.773
Well, the first
thing you might say

00:28:15.773 --> 00:28:17.940
is we'll go to the same
place where this Allan Hills

00:28:17.940 --> 00:28:20.480
rock came from and get
another one, get a better one.

00:28:20.480 --> 00:28:22.170
Well, people have identified--

00:28:22.170 --> 00:28:25.950
Nadine Barlow at Central Florida
has identified a place where

00:28:25.950 --> 00:28:27.420
this rock could have come from.

00:28:27.420 --> 00:28:29.970
She thinks it comes from
this crater right here,

00:28:29.970 --> 00:28:32.430
based on the fact that here is
a spot in the ancient crater

00:28:32.430 --> 00:28:33.340
terrain.

00:28:33.340 --> 00:28:35.940
The scale here is about
200 kilometers across.

00:28:35.940 --> 00:28:38.450
So these are large
craters, old craters.

00:28:38.450 --> 00:28:41.730
But there's one here, a fairly
young crater, only about 10,

00:28:41.730 --> 00:28:44.820
15 million years old, consistent
with the age of ejection

00:28:44.820 --> 00:28:46.170
for the Allan Hills rock.

00:28:46.170 --> 00:28:50.010
It's slightly oval, indicating
a side or a glancing impact,

00:28:50.010 --> 00:28:52.920
which is consistent with a
higher efficiency for ejecting

00:28:52.920 --> 00:28:53.627
material.

00:28:53.627 --> 00:28:54.460
So this could be it.

00:28:54.460 --> 00:28:57.330
Maybe the thing to do is
to direct our sample return

00:28:57.330 --> 00:28:59.400
mission to land
right in that crater,

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:01.920
drill down, and pull up, and
bring back another rock just

00:29:01.920 --> 00:29:03.282
like the one we got.

00:29:03.282 --> 00:29:04.740
Well, maybe that's
not a good idea,

00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:07.610
because we might find just like
the one we've got, only more.

00:29:07.610 --> 00:29:09.620
We haven't resolved the
question any better.

00:29:09.620 --> 00:29:11.370
Are there better places
to search for life

00:29:11.370 --> 00:29:13.590
than this type of material?

00:29:13.590 --> 00:29:14.560
I think there could be.

00:29:14.560 --> 00:29:16.982
And so I want to develop
a logic for a place where

00:29:16.982 --> 00:29:18.690
I think would be the
best place to search

00:29:18.690 --> 00:29:20.970
for evidence of
past life on Mars.

00:29:20.970 --> 00:29:24.450
And this logic is based on
going on places on Earth which

00:29:24.450 --> 00:29:25.440
are very Mars-like.

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:28.080
So what is the most
Mars-like place on Earth?

00:29:28.080 --> 00:29:30.105
It's the dry valleys
of the Antarctic.

00:29:30.105 --> 00:29:30.990
Let me show you.

00:29:30.990 --> 00:29:35.760
This is a Landsat photograph
looking down in the Antarctic.

00:29:35.760 --> 00:29:38.910
The scale here is
about 300 miles across.

00:29:38.910 --> 00:29:41.020
This is Ross Island,
this island here.

00:29:41.020 --> 00:29:44.370
This is Mount Erebus, the
active volcano in Antarctica.

00:29:44.370 --> 00:29:48.160
This is the main United States
station, McMurdo station.

00:29:48.160 --> 00:29:50.070
And you can see this
icebreaker track coming

00:29:50.070 --> 00:29:53.460
in carrying in Cheerios and milk
and other important supplies

00:29:53.460 --> 00:29:54.547
for the base there.

00:29:54.547 --> 00:29:56.880
But the reason we're-- the
region we're interested in is

00:29:56.880 --> 00:29:58.410
this region here,
the dry valleys.

00:29:58.410 --> 00:30:00.930
This is the coldest,
driest place on Earth.

00:30:00.930 --> 00:30:03.930
The mean annual temperature
is minus 20 centigrade,

00:30:03.930 --> 00:30:07.462
and the precipitation
here is tiny

00:30:07.462 --> 00:30:09.420
compared to the amount
of snow we have outside.

00:30:09.420 --> 00:30:12.180
It averages only
snow, only snow,

00:30:12.180 --> 00:30:15.210
and it averages about 2
centimeters equivalent water

00:30:15.210 --> 00:30:16.320
a year, which is--

00:30:16.320 --> 00:30:20.460
which is, for example, 20 times
drier than the Gobi Desert.

00:30:20.460 --> 00:30:25.350
This is an extremely dry,
extremely cold environment.

00:30:25.350 --> 00:30:27.360
Needless to say, in
this environment,

00:30:27.360 --> 00:30:28.860
there's hardly anything alive.

00:30:28.860 --> 00:30:31.440
In fact, looking down in
these valleys, it's lifeless.

00:30:31.440 --> 00:30:33.690
No birds, no
insects, no anything.

00:30:33.690 --> 00:30:35.230
It's dead as a doornail.

00:30:35.230 --> 00:30:38.040
I always take a doornail
with me just to be sure.

00:30:38.040 --> 00:30:39.180
Dead as a doornail.

00:30:39.180 --> 00:30:40.860
But on the floor
of these valleys,

00:30:40.860 --> 00:30:42.800
there's a big sheet of ice.

00:30:42.800 --> 00:30:44.820
Here's [? it, ?] about
5 kilometers across.

00:30:44.820 --> 00:30:46.603
A big sheet of ice.

00:30:46.603 --> 00:30:47.520
That's not surprising.

00:30:47.520 --> 00:30:49.650
This is-- I mean, the annual
temperature, minus 20,

00:30:49.650 --> 00:30:51.400
ice is not surprising.

00:30:51.400 --> 00:30:54.180
But what's surprising is that
underneath that sheet of ice,

00:30:54.180 --> 00:30:55.270
there's water.

00:30:55.270 --> 00:30:57.930
So what we do is we go down
there and we melt a big hole

00:30:57.930 --> 00:30:59.340
in that ice.

00:30:59.340 --> 00:31:03.710
It turns out it's about a little
bit less thick than this room.

00:31:03.710 --> 00:31:06.940
It varies, but we melt a hole
through the ice, about that big

00:31:06.940 --> 00:31:09.602
a hole, by heating up
some ethylene glycol

00:31:09.602 --> 00:31:11.310
and pumping it through
some copper coils.

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:13.560
As it melts down, we've
got a [INAUDIBLE] sump pump

00:31:13.560 --> 00:31:15.858
that just sits on it
and sprays the water out

00:31:15.858 --> 00:31:16.650
on the ice surface.

00:31:16.650 --> 00:31:18.390
We get the hole
through, and then we

00:31:18.390 --> 00:31:20.590
take some nonessential
federal employees--

00:31:20.590 --> 00:31:21.090
[LAUGHTER]

00:31:21.090 --> 00:31:23.400
--which we have a lot of.

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:25.600
Tie them to a rope
and lower them down.

00:31:25.600 --> 00:31:27.540
Say, what do you
find down there?

00:31:27.540 --> 00:31:30.630
Well, what we find down there
in the water underneath this ice

00:31:30.630 --> 00:31:32.760
is that there's algae there.

00:31:32.760 --> 00:31:35.490
There's life there, the
same sort of cyanobacterial

00:31:35.490 --> 00:31:38.730
mats that we think
populated the early Earth.

00:31:38.730 --> 00:31:41.430
So here in the most Mars-like
environment on Earth

00:31:41.430 --> 00:31:44.250
we find liquid water that's
persisting all year round.

00:31:44.250 --> 00:31:48.510
Some of these lakes can be
30 meters deep or deeper

00:31:48.510 --> 00:31:50.310
underneath, now,
relatively thin ice

00:31:50.310 --> 00:31:52.620
covers, persisting all
year round with life in it.

00:31:52.620 --> 00:31:54.340
Well, this is pretty amazing.

00:31:54.340 --> 00:31:56.533
And the first question
that we tried to address

00:31:56.533 --> 00:31:58.950
was, well, how can there be
liquid water environment where

00:31:58.950 --> 00:32:01.780
the mean annual
temperature is minus 20?

00:32:01.780 --> 00:32:03.780
And how can the
life survive there?

00:32:03.780 --> 00:32:05.740
And then what does this
tell us about Mars?

00:32:05.740 --> 00:32:07.740
So we tried to develop
models of this.

00:32:07.740 --> 00:32:09.665
And I won't bore you
with the details,

00:32:09.665 --> 00:32:11.040
but I'll just
quickly go through,

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:13.770
as sort of a fun
sophomore physics

00:32:13.770 --> 00:32:15.450
exercise in thermodynamics.

00:32:15.450 --> 00:32:16.440
There's energy sources.

00:32:16.440 --> 00:32:17.565
There's sunlight coming in.

00:32:17.565 --> 00:32:19.040
There could be geothermal heat.

00:32:19.040 --> 00:32:20.150
It's not very important.

00:32:20.150 --> 00:32:21.900
Turns out the geothermal
heat in this area

00:32:21.900 --> 00:32:25.470
is the global average, some 70
milliwatts per square meter,

00:32:25.470 --> 00:32:26.820
nothing to write home about.

00:32:26.820 --> 00:32:30.960
Sunlight comes through, enough
to photosynthesize, about 1%

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:32.483
transmission.

00:32:32.483 --> 00:32:34.650
10 to the minus 2, 10 to
the minus 3, but not enough

00:32:34.650 --> 00:32:35.900
to be appreciable heat source.

00:32:35.900 --> 00:32:41.100
It turns out the heat source is
the summer melt. What happens

00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:43.830
is that, indeed, the mean
annual temperature is minus 20.

00:32:43.830 --> 00:32:45.840
Let me show you a plot
of the temperature

00:32:45.840 --> 00:32:48.300
out-- throughout the
year at a site like this.

00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:49.890
This is a summer,
winter, summer.

00:32:49.890 --> 00:32:52.020
Summer, the sun's shining.

00:32:52.020 --> 00:32:53.400
January, it's quite warm.

00:32:53.400 --> 00:32:56.640
Temperatures can be a few
degrees above freezing, which

00:32:56.640 --> 00:32:58.680
for Antarctica is very warm.

00:32:58.680 --> 00:33:00.660
And then when winter
comes, the sun goes away.

00:33:00.660 --> 00:33:01.440
It gets very cold.

00:33:01.440 --> 00:33:03.447
Indeed, the average is minus 20.

00:33:03.447 --> 00:33:05.280
But what's important
here is that in summer,

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:07.440
for a couple days each
year, the temperature

00:33:07.440 --> 00:33:09.090
climbs above freezing.

00:33:09.090 --> 00:33:11.837
When that happens, go
back to this picture.

00:33:11.837 --> 00:33:12.920
You might have noticed it.

00:33:12.920 --> 00:33:14.968
When that happens,
the water, the ice

00:33:14.968 --> 00:33:17.510
in these glaciers surrounding
the valleys melts and the water

00:33:17.510 --> 00:33:20.750
flows down as
liquid water into--

00:33:20.750 --> 00:33:22.250
underneath the ice cover.

00:33:22.250 --> 00:33:25.250
And what is it--
when water is melted,

00:33:25.250 --> 00:33:29.510
it has the latent heat of
fusion, which for water is

00:33:29.510 --> 00:33:31.640
enormous, 80 calories per gram.

00:33:31.640 --> 00:33:33.710
And in order for the--

00:33:33.710 --> 00:33:37.010
that water to freeze, it has
to release that latent heat,

00:33:37.010 --> 00:33:39.620
and that latent heat has to
be conducted through the ice

00:33:39.620 --> 00:33:42.140
cover.

00:33:42.140 --> 00:33:44.490
And it's that
light and heat that

00:33:44.490 --> 00:33:47.040
forms the energy source
that keeps the lake liquid.

00:33:47.040 --> 00:33:48.690
So what's essential
for understanding

00:33:48.690 --> 00:33:52.350
this environment is that the
temperature, not that the mean

00:33:52.350 --> 00:33:54.720
annual is below freezing,
but that the temperature gets

00:33:54.720 --> 00:33:57.503
above freezing for a
little bit each year.

00:33:57.503 --> 00:33:59.670
The mean annual temperature
determines the thickness

00:33:59.670 --> 00:34:02.430
of the ice, of course, because
that determines the barrier.

00:34:02.430 --> 00:34:03.930
What it can do to
the thermodynamics

00:34:03.930 --> 00:34:06.510
is very simple, because
the ice-water interface

00:34:06.510 --> 00:34:09.389
is at 0 degrees, by definition.

00:34:09.389 --> 00:34:11.159
The mean annual is minus 20.

00:34:11.159 --> 00:34:13.440
Heat is being lost
by conduction.

00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:14.610
Energy comes in, sunlight.

00:34:14.610 --> 00:34:15.360
That's negligible.

00:34:15.360 --> 00:34:16.530
Geothermal heat, negligible.

00:34:16.530 --> 00:34:18.177
This is the latent heat flux.

00:34:18.177 --> 00:34:20.219
And you can very simply
integrate that and soften

00:34:20.219 --> 00:34:21.810
the thickness of the ice.

00:34:21.810 --> 00:34:23.670
And sure enough, it
works in Antarctica.

00:34:23.670 --> 00:34:26.520
We can then model it on Mars
and say, how long could lakes

00:34:26.520 --> 00:34:28.290
like this persist on Mars?

00:34:28.290 --> 00:34:30.449
And indeed, we
could show that long

00:34:30.449 --> 00:34:34.290
after the rest of the
planet is dead, frozen, cold

00:34:34.290 --> 00:34:36.810
like Antarctica, there
could be ice covered lakes

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:39.780
like the Antarctic, down to mean
annual temperatures on Mars,

00:34:39.780 --> 00:34:41.610
down to minus 35.

00:34:41.610 --> 00:34:43.230
We've done some
simple climate models

00:34:43.230 --> 00:34:44.897
that suggest that
this time period could

00:34:44.897 --> 00:34:47.080
be on the order of
half a billion years.

00:34:47.080 --> 00:34:49.710
So life could have existed
in ice-covered lakes on Mars,

00:34:49.710 --> 00:34:51.900
like in Antarctica,
half a billion years

00:34:51.900 --> 00:34:54.270
after the rest of the
planet would have been dead.

00:34:54.270 --> 00:34:55.350
Are there lakes on Mars?

00:34:55.350 --> 00:34:57.517
Can we find spots where
there would have been lakes?

00:34:57.517 --> 00:35:00.280
Well, let me show you my
favorite spot on Mars.

00:35:00.280 --> 00:35:04.890
This is a canyon, Hebes Chasma
in the Valles Marineris canyon

00:35:04.890 --> 00:35:06.300
system near the equator.

00:35:06.300 --> 00:35:08.550
Hebe was one of
the Greek pantheon.

00:35:08.550 --> 00:35:09.978
And this is her canyon.

00:35:09.978 --> 00:35:11.520
And in the middle
of the canyon, it's

00:35:11.520 --> 00:35:12.812
a-- you see the scale bar here.

00:35:12.812 --> 00:35:14.640
It's several hundred
kilometers long

00:35:14.640 --> 00:35:16.710
on the order of a
hundred kilometers wide.

00:35:16.710 --> 00:35:19.823
It's got-- in the middle of
the canyon, there's a plateau,

00:35:19.823 --> 00:35:21.240
and if you look
carefully, you can

00:35:21.240 --> 00:35:23.940
see that the plateau has
laminations on the side.

00:35:23.940 --> 00:35:26.850
We think that this
canyon was full of water

00:35:26.850 --> 00:35:30.270
and that this plateau is
sediments that were deposited,

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:32.520
possibly carbonate sediments,
that were deposited when

00:35:32.520 --> 00:35:33.910
that canyon was full of water.

00:35:33.910 --> 00:35:36.990
There's evidence that suggests
that this whole region

00:35:36.990 --> 00:35:39.750
experienced extensive
groundwater flow, as water

00:35:39.750 --> 00:35:42.720
drained from Tharsis
through this region down

00:35:42.720 --> 00:35:44.065
onto the northern plains.

00:35:44.065 --> 00:35:46.440
So there's reason to think
there is extensive groundwater

00:35:46.440 --> 00:35:48.482
flow in here, would have
been filled with a lake.

00:35:48.482 --> 00:35:50.607
This deep canyon would have
been filled [? with. ?]

00:35:50.607 --> 00:35:52.500
The lake was a relatively
thin ice cover,

00:35:52.500 --> 00:35:55.500
even as temperatures on Mars
got down below minus 20,

00:35:55.500 --> 00:35:56.473
down to minus 35.

00:35:56.473 --> 00:35:58.140
There might have been
life in this lake.

00:35:58.140 --> 00:36:00.870
What we'd like to do is land
in the middle of this plateau,

00:36:00.870 --> 00:36:03.862
drill down to see if
there's any fossils.

00:36:03.862 --> 00:36:05.070
And there's two reasons here.

00:36:05.070 --> 00:36:07.200
One is a lake is a
good place to live.

00:36:07.200 --> 00:36:08.760
As I showed you
in the Antarctic,

00:36:08.760 --> 00:36:10.980
long after the rest
of the environment

00:36:10.980 --> 00:36:12.580
became too dry
and cold for life,

00:36:12.580 --> 00:36:14.190
there might have
been life in a lake.

00:36:14.190 --> 00:36:16.482
A lake, an ice covered-lake,
would've been a good place

00:36:16.482 --> 00:36:17.880
to live on a cold planet.

00:36:17.880 --> 00:36:20.160
But even more important,
a lake is a good place

00:36:20.160 --> 00:36:23.160
to die because if
something dies in the lake,

00:36:23.160 --> 00:36:25.608
it can get buried and
preserved in the sediments.

00:36:25.608 --> 00:36:27.900
Those river features I showed
you might have had water,

00:36:27.900 --> 00:36:29.733
and it might have been
great places to live,

00:36:29.733 --> 00:36:33.090
but it's not clear that they're
great places to find fossils.

00:36:33.090 --> 00:36:35.610
And I often drive
this point home

00:36:35.610 --> 00:36:38.340
by telling the story of
going to Los Angeles, which

00:36:38.340 --> 00:36:39.090
I don't recommend.

00:36:39.090 --> 00:36:41.898
But if you do go to Los Angeles,
to visit the La Brea Tar Pits.

00:36:41.898 --> 00:36:43.440
Here's this big tar
pits, and there's

00:36:43.440 --> 00:36:46.080
all sorts of fossils of saber
tooth tigers and wolves.

00:36:46.080 --> 00:36:48.352
And you think, why did
they live in tar pits?

00:36:48.352 --> 00:36:49.810
Well, they didn't
live in tar pits.

00:36:49.810 --> 00:36:51.852
It's just that tar pits
is where evidence of them

00:36:51.852 --> 00:36:52.920
were preserved.

00:36:52.920 --> 00:36:54.863
The same challenge is
going to be on Mars.

00:36:54.863 --> 00:36:57.030
It's not enough to find a
place where there is life.

00:36:57.030 --> 00:37:00.090
We have to find a place where
the evidence of that life

00:37:00.090 --> 00:37:01.055
is preserved.

00:37:01.055 --> 00:37:02.430
And in this case,
nature has been

00:37:02.430 --> 00:37:06.195
kind to us in that a lake bed,
I think, provides both examples.

00:37:06.195 --> 00:37:08.820
It provides an environment which
would have supported life long

00:37:08.820 --> 00:37:10.980
after the average,
and it also provides

00:37:10.980 --> 00:37:13.980
an excellent environment
for preserving evidence

00:37:13.980 --> 00:37:15.330
of that life as fossils.

00:37:15.330 --> 00:37:18.390
Unfortunately, they won't let
us land anywhere near this lake.

00:37:18.390 --> 00:37:21.480
You look at, for example, the
[? aero-lift ?] on Pathfinder.

00:37:21.480 --> 00:37:23.190
It's 160 kilometers.

00:37:23.190 --> 00:37:25.440
That's about like that,
you've got a reasonable chance

00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:27.980
of hitting a 5 kilometer
cliff and rolling down.

00:37:27.980 --> 00:37:30.870
And the project manager
doesn't like that.

00:37:30.870 --> 00:37:34.320
So they want us to find a lake
bed that's easier to get to,

00:37:34.320 --> 00:37:36.510
one that's near an airport
or something like that.

00:37:36.510 --> 00:37:38.280
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:38.280 --> 00:37:39.930
So we've been
trying to find that,

00:37:39.930 --> 00:37:42.500
and we think we've got one.

00:37:42.500 --> 00:37:43.980
And that's this one right here.

00:37:43.980 --> 00:37:45.780
This is our practical site.

00:37:45.780 --> 00:37:51.090
This is a lake, Gusev Crater,
in the southern ancient cratered

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:51.732
highlands.

00:37:51.732 --> 00:37:53.190
You can see from
the scale bar it's

00:37:53.190 --> 00:37:55.840
about 100 kilometers across.

00:37:55.840 --> 00:37:58.350
There's a river, Ma'adim
Valles, that flowed in.

00:37:58.350 --> 00:38:00.290
And we think these
sediments are off--

00:38:00.290 --> 00:38:03.060
basically deposited when
this canyon-- crater

00:38:03.060 --> 00:38:03.810
was full of water.

00:38:03.810 --> 00:38:07.440
We think this was a crater lake,
with deep sediments deposited

00:38:07.440 --> 00:38:09.552
when it was full of water.

00:38:09.552 --> 00:38:11.010
And it's much
smoother and flatter.

00:38:11.010 --> 00:38:11.820
No cliffs here.

00:38:11.820 --> 00:38:12.630
Easy target.

00:38:12.630 --> 00:38:14.160
Land in the middle of the Rover.

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:17.790
Drive around, head down, cross
the shoreline, go up the creek.

00:38:17.790 --> 00:38:19.860
We could be up the
creek on Mars and find

00:38:19.860 --> 00:38:21.270
what we're looking for.

00:38:21.270 --> 00:38:25.200
So this is our
current best example.

00:38:25.200 --> 00:38:26.940
And now I want to--

00:38:26.940 --> 00:38:28.082
so this will be--

00:38:28.082 --> 00:38:30.040
I want to emphasize a
point that in both cases,

00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:31.540
what we're searching
for is fossils.

00:38:31.540 --> 00:38:33.457
This is what was in the
Allan Hills meteorite,

00:38:33.457 --> 00:38:34.260
if we believe it.

00:38:34.260 --> 00:38:36.420
This is what we find
on rocks on Earth,

00:38:36.420 --> 00:38:37.973
3 and 1/2 billion years ago.

00:38:37.973 --> 00:38:39.390
And this is what
we'd be searching

00:38:39.390 --> 00:38:40.473
for in these ancient lake.

00:38:40.473 --> 00:38:42.700
That's fossils,
basically footprints.

00:38:42.700 --> 00:38:45.087
We don't actually find
the organism itself.

00:38:45.087 --> 00:38:46.045
We find the footprints.

00:38:46.045 --> 00:38:48.700
So I want to make the case
now that that's not enough.

00:38:48.700 --> 00:38:50.900
That would be interesting,
and it would make my day.

00:38:50.900 --> 00:38:53.050
It would make my
decade if we found

00:38:53.050 --> 00:38:55.185
really good fossil
evidence of life on Mars,

00:38:55.185 --> 00:38:56.560
but it's not
enough, and it's not

00:38:56.560 --> 00:38:58.330
the end of the story
in that we want

00:38:58.330 --> 00:38:59.830
to be able to find
something that we

00:38:59.830 --> 00:39:02.110
can compare to life on Earth.

00:39:02.110 --> 00:39:05.430
And I want to illustrate this
with a very interesting graphic

00:39:05.430 --> 00:39:06.805
that was published
in the Journal

00:39:06.805 --> 00:39:09.370
of Irreproducible Results,
which is a comparison of apples

00:39:09.370 --> 00:39:10.240
and oranges.

00:39:10.240 --> 00:39:11.890
People say you can't
compare apples and oranges--

00:39:11.890 --> 00:39:12.390
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:12.390 --> 00:39:15.560
--but in fact, you have to
compare apples and oranges.

00:39:15.560 --> 00:39:18.155
If you want to understand the
broader category of fruit,

00:39:18.155 --> 00:39:19.780
you have to compare
apples and oranges.

00:39:19.780 --> 00:39:23.260
In fact, you'd also want to
compare bananas, and even

00:39:23.260 --> 00:39:25.780
tomatoes, which,
legally, are vegetables,

00:39:25.780 --> 00:39:27.430
but biologically,
they're fruits.

00:39:27.430 --> 00:39:29.020
You want to compare them all.

00:39:29.020 --> 00:39:30.220
And this is the point.

00:39:30.220 --> 00:39:34.830
On Earth, we only have apples
in terms of life, or oranges.

00:39:34.830 --> 00:39:36.010
Well, we only have one.

00:39:36.010 --> 00:39:37.630
If you look at
life on Earth, you

00:39:37.630 --> 00:39:39.172
see that it's all
the same stuff.

00:39:39.172 --> 00:39:40.380
It's all genetically related.

00:39:40.380 --> 00:39:42.460
We have one example
of life on Earth.

00:39:42.460 --> 00:39:44.710
This is the 16S
ribosomal RNA tree

00:39:44.710 --> 00:39:49.130
of life from Carl Woese showing
the main families of life.

00:39:49.130 --> 00:39:51.342
These are the eukaryotes,
animals, plants, mushrooms,

00:39:51.342 --> 00:39:53.050
and all those sorts
of things are up here

00:39:53.050 --> 00:39:54.160
in the green branch.

00:39:54.160 --> 00:39:56.590
And then the bacteria form
two branches, the eubacteria

00:39:56.590 --> 00:39:58.030
and the archaeobacteria.

00:39:58.030 --> 00:39:59.710
All life on Earth is related.

00:39:59.710 --> 00:40:02.502
There's no reason, a
priori, why this had to be.

00:40:02.502 --> 00:40:04.210
We could have mapped
out the tree of life

00:40:04.210 --> 00:40:06.550
and found that there are
two distinct trees, two

00:40:06.550 --> 00:40:08.410
separate genesis
of life on Earth

00:40:08.410 --> 00:40:10.050
that have learned
to live together.

00:40:10.050 --> 00:40:11.320
Ha, ha, right?

00:40:11.320 --> 00:40:12.670
But it's not.

00:40:12.670 --> 00:40:13.360
It's only one.

00:40:13.360 --> 00:40:15.230
If there was somebody
else, we ate them.

00:40:15.230 --> 00:40:15.505
They're gone.

00:40:15.505 --> 00:40:16.005
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:16.005 --> 00:40:18.740
All that's left is
one example of life.

00:40:18.740 --> 00:40:20.410
And this is a profound--

00:40:20.410 --> 00:40:22.790
it's what's called the
unity of biochemistry.

00:40:22.790 --> 00:40:26.500
All life on Earth has the same
DNA, RNA code, the same 20

00:40:26.500 --> 00:40:28.150
left-handed amino acids.

00:40:28.150 --> 00:40:29.450
It's all the same.

00:40:29.450 --> 00:40:31.660
Well, this is what I
mean by one data point.

00:40:31.660 --> 00:40:34.120
If we want to understand
life as a general phenomenon,

00:40:34.120 --> 00:40:36.400
we've got to be able to
get life from somewhere

00:40:36.400 --> 00:40:40.480
else and sequence its ribosomal
RNA, if it's even got it,

00:40:40.480 --> 00:40:42.130
look at its amino acids.

00:40:42.130 --> 00:40:43.490
Fossils are not enough.

00:40:43.490 --> 00:40:45.648
We need actual bodies.

00:40:45.648 --> 00:40:47.440
We need to be able--
something that's alive

00:40:47.440 --> 00:40:51.550
or something that's dead, but
still preserved organically.

00:40:51.550 --> 00:40:54.220
Well, there's a possibility
that the life could be survive--

00:40:54.220 --> 00:40:55.390
still survive on Mars.

00:40:55.390 --> 00:40:56.280
Not on the surface.

00:40:56.280 --> 00:40:57.280
It's way too dry.

00:40:57.280 --> 00:40:59.560
But maybe under-- deep
under the surface,

00:40:59.560 --> 00:41:02.770
there might be a magma source
associated with ground ice

00:41:02.770 --> 00:41:06.410
forming liquid water and
providing an energy source.

00:41:06.410 --> 00:41:12.400
For example, hydrogen and
CO2, for which methanogens

00:41:12.400 --> 00:41:14.830
could be the basis
of an ecosystem.

00:41:14.830 --> 00:41:17.620
And we're now finding these
on Earth, interestingly.

00:41:17.620 --> 00:41:22.028
So this diagram actually was
published about six years ago.

00:41:22.028 --> 00:41:24.070
And since then, people
are now finding these kind

00:41:24.070 --> 00:41:26.410
of systems completely
isolated from the surface

00:41:26.410 --> 00:41:29.320
of the Earth, subsurface
microbial ecosystems.

00:41:29.320 --> 00:41:31.510
Many microbial ecosystems
on Earth that we think

00:41:31.510 --> 00:41:33.100
of as being isolated are not.

00:41:33.100 --> 00:41:35.080
For example, the deep
sea vents, because they

00:41:35.080 --> 00:41:38.810
rely on oxygen from the upper
atmosphere-- from the surface.

00:41:38.810 --> 00:41:41.140
But we're now finding ones
that are truly anaerobic,

00:41:41.140 --> 00:41:43.960
chemoautotropic systems in
the subsurface on Earth.

00:41:43.960 --> 00:41:45.520
There's been at least one.

00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:48.167
And so it's possible that
deep underground on Mars,

00:41:48.167 --> 00:41:49.000
life is still there.

00:41:49.000 --> 00:41:52.480
We can dig it up, drag it here,
and analyze it, and see if it

00:41:52.480 --> 00:41:56.590
maps onto our tree of life.

00:41:56.590 --> 00:41:57.730
Why would we care about it?

00:41:57.730 --> 00:41:59.313
Why was it possible
that it might just

00:41:59.313 --> 00:42:03.580
map onto another diagram here,
is that, as I was saying,

00:42:03.580 --> 00:42:08.050
Earth and Mars were habitable
early in their history

00:42:08.050 --> 00:42:10.537
when there was a lot
of meteorite impact,

00:42:10.537 --> 00:42:12.370
and there could have
been extensive exchange

00:42:12.370 --> 00:42:14.753
of meteorites between the
two planets at that time,

00:42:14.753 --> 00:42:16.170
during the late
heavy bombardment.

00:42:16.170 --> 00:42:18.520
And we know, or we think,
that there was life at Earth--

00:42:18.520 --> 00:42:19.805
on Earth at that time.

00:42:19.805 --> 00:42:21.430
So the planets, early
in their history,

00:42:21.430 --> 00:42:24.370
might literally
been swapping spit

00:42:24.370 --> 00:42:27.260
and sending microorganisms
back and forth.

00:42:27.260 --> 00:42:30.340
We're learning that maybe the
planets are not biologically

00:42:30.340 --> 00:42:32.630
isolated the way we
thought they were.

00:42:32.630 --> 00:42:35.448
And so it could be that we go
to Mars and find the organisms.

00:42:35.448 --> 00:42:37.240
Maybe they're still
alive, and they're just

00:42:37.240 --> 00:42:40.570
another branch on the
eubacterial or archaeobacterial

00:42:40.570 --> 00:42:41.153
tree.

00:42:41.153 --> 00:42:42.820
Well, I don't think
it's actually likely

00:42:42.820 --> 00:42:45.280
that we'll find
subsurface life on Mars.

00:42:45.280 --> 00:42:46.998
I think it's highly unlikely.

00:42:46.998 --> 00:42:49.540
But I think there's another way
we might get at this question

00:42:49.540 --> 00:42:53.630
by actually finding the remains
of frozen Martian organisms.

00:42:53.630 --> 00:42:57.010
And that's, again, based
on a terrestrial example.

00:42:57.010 --> 00:42:59.860
In this case, it's the
Siberian permafrost.

00:42:59.860 --> 00:43:04.570
Here in Siberia is some of the
oldest frozen ground on Earth.

00:43:04.570 --> 00:43:06.520
This is the Kolyma region.

00:43:06.520 --> 00:43:09.370
There's a river here, the Kolyma
river that flows, cutting away

00:43:09.370 --> 00:43:10.250
this permafrost.

00:43:10.250 --> 00:43:11.570
And I'm showing you here.

00:43:11.570 --> 00:43:13.915
This is the active
zone, first meter

00:43:13.915 --> 00:43:15.850
or so, which thaws each summer.

00:43:15.850 --> 00:43:18.730
But below that is the
permanently frozen ground.

00:43:18.730 --> 00:43:21.880
This ground has been frozen
for 3 and 1/2 million years.

00:43:21.880 --> 00:43:24.653
We know the age from geomagnetic
reversals in the sediments,

00:43:24.653 --> 00:43:26.320
and we know it's been
permanently frozen

00:43:26.320 --> 00:43:28.840
from the geomorphology
of these ice wedges.

00:43:28.840 --> 00:43:31.730
These are solid ice wedges here.

00:43:31.730 --> 00:43:34.630
And what we do is we get
with our Russian colleagues,

00:43:34.630 --> 00:43:36.820
go back away from that
cliff, and drill down

00:43:36.820 --> 00:43:38.120
into the permafrost.

00:43:38.120 --> 00:43:39.888
And we drill without
drilling fluids,

00:43:39.888 --> 00:43:42.180
which is done because the
drill would get stuck readily

00:43:42.180 --> 00:43:44.070
if you do that, but
it allows us to drill

00:43:44.070 --> 00:43:46.350
with sterile technique, so
that the core we pull out,

00:43:46.350 --> 00:43:49.140
the inside of that core,
is uncontaminated, still

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:49.950
frozen and clean.

00:43:49.950 --> 00:43:51.900
We break it open,
look for organisms.

00:43:51.900 --> 00:43:55.110
We find that there's still
viable bacteria frozen

00:43:55.110 --> 00:43:57.960
in this ground for 3
and 1/2 million years.

00:43:57.960 --> 00:44:00.260
3 and 1/2 million years
frozen and still there.

00:44:00.260 --> 00:44:01.980
Temperature is minus 10.

00:44:01.980 --> 00:44:03.760
Well, what does
that imply for Mars?

00:44:03.760 --> 00:44:06.150
Well, Siberia,
frozen in the ground,

00:44:06.150 --> 00:44:09.070
minus 10, 3 million years.

00:44:09.070 --> 00:44:09.840
Question, then.

00:44:09.840 --> 00:44:12.790
Is there freeze-dried
life on Mars?

00:44:12.790 --> 00:44:16.113
Well, on Mars, we need it to
be frozen for 3 billion years.

00:44:16.113 --> 00:44:17.280
Well, billions and millions.

00:44:17.280 --> 00:44:19.200
They sound alike, but there's
actually a big difference--

00:44:19.200 --> 00:44:19.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:19.770 --> 00:44:22.530
--between them, a
factor of 1,000.

00:44:22.530 --> 00:44:24.690
Tell that to your congressman.

00:44:24.690 --> 00:44:27.040
But it's also a
lot colder on Mars.

00:44:27.040 --> 00:44:29.050
It's minus 70
instead of minus 10.

00:44:29.050 --> 00:44:31.722
That's 60 degrees colder.

00:44:31.722 --> 00:44:33.930
Well, you could do sort of
a handwaving argument that

00:44:33.930 --> 00:44:38.100
says that the Q10 of biological
degradation at low temperatures

00:44:38.100 --> 00:44:39.165
is about 3.

00:44:39.165 --> 00:44:41.790
In other words, it would change
in reactivity rate for every 10

00:44:41.790 --> 00:44:42.290
degrees.

00:44:42.290 --> 00:44:44.040
Drop in temperature is about 3.

00:44:44.040 --> 00:44:45.710
60 degrees in temperature.

00:44:45.710 --> 00:44:48.990
3 to the 6 very niftily
turns millions into billions,

00:44:48.990 --> 00:44:51.540
and you could argue
that if life can survive

00:44:51.540 --> 00:44:53.790
being frozen for 3
million at minus 10,

00:44:53.790 --> 00:44:55.890
then it could survive
being frozen for 3 billion

00:44:55.890 --> 00:44:56.970
at minus 70.

00:44:56.970 --> 00:44:59.190
And we'll dig up these
frozen Mars organisms

00:44:59.190 --> 00:45:02.400
from the south polar region,
and they will be alive.

00:45:02.400 --> 00:45:05.010
Well, the answer turns
out to be no, not because

00:45:05.010 --> 00:45:06.790
of this thermodynamic argument.

00:45:06.790 --> 00:45:08.370
Thermodynamics works
in their favor,

00:45:08.370 --> 00:45:11.100
but because of radiation.

00:45:11.100 --> 00:45:12.880
Last year we were in Siberia.

00:45:12.880 --> 00:45:15.480
And I'll never go again, because
the [? Aeroflot ?] airlines

00:45:15.480 --> 00:45:17.650
are really coming apart and--

00:45:17.650 --> 00:45:18.520
seriously.

00:45:18.520 --> 00:45:20.940
But after we drilled
a hole, we lowered

00:45:20.940 --> 00:45:23.400
a specially constructed
Geiger counter just

00:45:23.400 --> 00:45:25.770
to measure the naturally
occurring radioactivity.

00:45:25.770 --> 00:45:28.170
And this is windblown
sediment in origin.

00:45:28.170 --> 00:45:30.120
There's nothing
particularly dangerous

00:45:30.120 --> 00:45:32.490
here in terms of concentrations
of [? radionuclides ?]

00:45:32.490 --> 00:45:35.790
but just the naturally-occurring
uranium, thorium, potassium,

00:45:35.790 --> 00:45:38.940
just crustal average
abundances, part per million,

00:45:38.940 --> 00:45:42.900
generate radioactivity here such
that a lethal dose of radiation

00:45:42.900 --> 00:45:45.348
is accumulated in
about 10 million years.

00:45:45.348 --> 00:45:47.640
If you were to lie in this
permafrost settlement for 10

00:45:47.640 --> 00:45:49.800
million years, you
would be dead, for sure.

00:45:49.800 --> 00:45:50.340
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:50.340 --> 00:45:51.720
Frozen or not.

00:45:51.720 --> 00:45:54.720
And while on Earth we're looking
at survival for 3 millions,

00:45:54.720 --> 00:45:56.070
it's not a big problem.

00:45:56.070 --> 00:45:58.052
We'd expect some
radio selectivity

00:45:58.052 --> 00:45:59.760
among the microorganisms,
but we wouldn't

00:45:59.760 --> 00:46:01.200
expect it to be sterile.

00:46:01.200 --> 00:46:03.250
But on Mars, where
the crustal abundances

00:46:03.250 --> 00:46:07.350
of these radioisotopes is
going to be roughly the same,

00:46:07.350 --> 00:46:09.270
organisms preserved
for 3 billion

00:46:09.270 --> 00:46:13.080
will have accumulated hundreds
of lethal doses of radiation.

00:46:13.080 --> 00:46:14.667
They will be dead
as doornails, again.

00:46:14.667 --> 00:46:16.500
We'll have that same
doornail in our pocket,

00:46:16.500 --> 00:46:17.333
and they'll be dead.

00:46:17.333 --> 00:46:19.020
Which actually, might
be a good thing.

00:46:19.020 --> 00:46:21.510
We're digging up Martian
organisms, warming them up.

00:46:21.510 --> 00:46:22.920
Who knows what they'd be like?

00:46:22.920 --> 00:46:24.540
We might be glad
that they're dead.

00:46:24.540 --> 00:46:26.706
They'll be dead, but
they'll still be there.

00:46:26.706 --> 00:46:29.880
Their proteins, if they've got
proteins, will still be intact.

00:46:29.880 --> 00:46:32.340
We can grind them up and
run them through a GC

00:46:32.340 --> 00:46:35.580
and see if they've got the same
20 amino acids that we have.

00:46:35.580 --> 00:46:37.590
We could sequence
their ribosomal RNA,

00:46:37.590 --> 00:46:39.080
if they even got ribosomes.

00:46:39.080 --> 00:46:42.030
If they've got proteins,
they should have ribosomes.

00:46:42.030 --> 00:46:43.920
We could compare
them biochemically.

00:46:43.920 --> 00:46:46.620
We could get a lot further
in our understanding of life

00:46:46.620 --> 00:46:48.750
than we would just
by having a fossil,

00:46:48.750 --> 00:46:53.130
because we'd actually have an
organism that we could examine.

00:46:53.130 --> 00:46:57.477
And so I argue that finding
a fossil would be exciting,

00:46:57.477 --> 00:46:59.560
but there will be exciting
things to do after that

00:46:59.560 --> 00:47:01.750
as well that will address
fundamental questions.

00:47:01.750 --> 00:47:03.280
Let me now turn a little
bit to the missions

00:47:03.280 --> 00:47:04.780
that are coming
up, just to remind

00:47:04.780 --> 00:47:08.020
you some of the exciting
things that we have in store.

00:47:08.020 --> 00:47:11.350
Right now, this is actually a
photo of Mars Observer, which

00:47:11.350 --> 00:47:15.220
didn't make it to Mars,
but its reconstructed copy

00:47:15.220 --> 00:47:18.700
will be on-- reaching Mars
later this year, on July 4th.

00:47:18.700 --> 00:47:21.130
Pathfinder will land, the
little Rover that will go out.

00:47:21.130 --> 00:47:23.547
Neither of these will really
address the kind of questions

00:47:23.547 --> 00:47:24.610
I've been talking about.

00:47:24.610 --> 00:47:26.080
Mars Observer will
indeed give us

00:47:26.080 --> 00:47:28.090
better imaging of
the planet, and so we

00:47:28.090 --> 00:47:32.200
could do more refined analysis
and more detailed speculation

00:47:32.200 --> 00:47:34.795
about lakes and places that
we might want to go to.

00:47:34.795 --> 00:47:36.670
Ultimately, we'll want
to go to such a place,

00:47:36.670 --> 00:47:40.060
and the idea now is to construct
a Rover, maybe something

00:47:40.060 --> 00:47:44.650
like this [INAUDIBLE],, maybe
a derivative of the Sojourner

00:47:44.650 --> 00:47:46.240
Rover.

00:47:46.240 --> 00:47:48.280
But this will be the first
time to really direct

00:47:48.280 --> 00:47:50.488
some of these questions,
land on an ancient lake bed,

00:47:50.488 --> 00:47:53.028
with something, some kind
of arm that can drill down,

00:47:53.028 --> 00:47:55.570
or some kind of drill that could
go down and pull up a sample

00:47:55.570 --> 00:47:58.810
and see if there's carbonates
and organic material, and maybe

00:47:58.810 --> 00:48:00.310
even fossils tied in.

00:48:00.310 --> 00:48:03.490
But I argue that ultimately,
they're-- the questions here

00:48:03.490 --> 00:48:07.270
are big enough and the problem
is deep enough that this is

00:48:07.270 --> 00:48:09.490
going to be what
humans do when they go.

00:48:09.490 --> 00:48:11.140
And so this is--

00:48:11.140 --> 00:48:13.480
I've become an advocate
of human exploration,

00:48:13.480 --> 00:48:16.580
because I think humans bring a
unique skill to this problem.

00:48:16.580 --> 00:48:18.710
And the only way I can
describe it is by analogy,

00:48:18.710 --> 00:48:20.440
and it's an imperfect
analogy, I realize.

00:48:20.440 --> 00:48:22.505
But suppose you find
yourself standing

00:48:22.505 --> 00:48:24.130
in front of a crowd
of a hundred people

00:48:24.130 --> 00:48:25.720
or so, like in this
room, and one of them

00:48:25.720 --> 00:48:27.928
happen to be, say, your
mother sitting in the middle.

00:48:27.928 --> 00:48:30.970
You'd be very instantly
able to pick that face out,

00:48:30.970 --> 00:48:34.890
that familiar face out of this
whole sea of unfamiliar faces.

00:48:34.890 --> 00:48:37.120
It's just something
that we're intrinsically

00:48:37.120 --> 00:48:39.070
capable of doing,
recognizing patterns

00:48:39.070 --> 00:48:41.510
and biological systems.

00:48:41.510 --> 00:48:43.892
Another example is you have
too little black animals.

00:48:43.892 --> 00:48:45.100
One's a dog, and one's a cat.

00:48:45.100 --> 00:48:46.433
How can you tell the difference?

00:48:46.433 --> 00:48:47.540
Well, it's very easy.

00:48:47.540 --> 00:48:49.298
If you're a human,
you know right away.

00:48:49.298 --> 00:48:50.590
Somebody once suggested a task.

00:48:50.590 --> 00:48:51.420
You throw a stick.

00:48:51.420 --> 00:48:52.920
The one that runs
after the stick is

00:48:52.920 --> 00:48:54.730
a dog, and the one that looks
at you like you're crazy

00:48:54.730 --> 00:48:55.300
is the cat.

00:48:55.300 --> 00:48:55.820
[LAUGHTER]

00:48:55.820 --> 00:48:58.390
But we as humans are
very, very capable

00:48:58.390 --> 00:49:00.130
of doing that kind
of recognition.

00:49:00.130 --> 00:49:02.200
And yet it would be very
hard, at least for me,

00:49:02.200 --> 00:49:04.252
to imagine programming
a machine to make

00:49:04.252 --> 00:49:05.710
that kind of
recognition or to make

00:49:05.710 --> 00:49:07.040
that kind of differentiation.

00:49:07.040 --> 00:49:10.870
So I argue, and I met without
a real hard justification,

00:49:10.870 --> 00:49:15.010
that humans bring a unique skill
to the recognition of life,

00:49:15.010 --> 00:49:18.160
even on Mars, and that that
will be their major contribution

00:49:18.160 --> 00:49:22.600
and their major task to
the exploring of Mars.

00:49:22.600 --> 00:49:26.170
And let me take off from
that, if humans go to Mars,

00:49:26.170 --> 00:49:28.570
set up research bases,
they'll certainly--

00:49:28.570 --> 00:49:30.460
as is imagined here--

00:49:30.460 --> 00:49:31.780
be rather self-sufficient.

00:49:31.780 --> 00:49:34.030
Their oxygen will come from
the CO2 in the atmosphere

00:49:34.030 --> 00:49:36.640
and the nitrogen for buffer
gas for breathable air

00:49:36.640 --> 00:49:37.990
will come from the atmosphere.

00:49:37.990 --> 00:49:39.520
Water will come
from the atmosphere.

00:49:39.520 --> 00:49:40.990
They'll be fairly
self-sufficient

00:49:40.990 --> 00:49:42.810
out of necessity from Earth.

00:49:42.810 --> 00:49:45.790
And that, I think, leads to
the question of the long term

00:49:45.790 --> 00:49:46.450
future.

00:49:46.450 --> 00:49:48.040
What is the long-term
future of Mars?

00:49:48.040 --> 00:49:49.270
What does it hold?

00:49:49.270 --> 00:49:52.090
And it also goes back to water.

00:49:52.090 --> 00:49:54.530
I've been arguing that Mars
is a planet with a past,

00:49:54.530 --> 00:49:56.600
a steamy, wet past.

00:49:56.600 --> 00:49:58.940
We have some ideas of how
it got to the present.

00:49:58.940 --> 00:50:00.970
We're interested in who
is alive in the past.

00:50:00.970 --> 00:50:03.040
But does Mars have a future?

00:50:03.040 --> 00:50:05.380
Could we bring it back to life?

00:50:05.380 --> 00:50:08.522
Could we do CPR on
this dead planet?

00:50:08.522 --> 00:50:09.730
Well, what would be required?

00:50:09.730 --> 00:50:11.605
The first order what
you one would have to do

00:50:11.605 --> 00:50:13.030
is warm up the planet.

00:50:13.030 --> 00:50:14.687
But we know how to
warm up planets.

00:50:14.687 --> 00:50:15.770
We're doing that on Earth.

00:50:15.770 --> 00:50:16.270
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:16.270 --> 00:50:19.460
And in fact, if we would produce
the same sort of gases methane,

00:50:19.460 --> 00:50:22.570
ammonia, nitrous oxide,
chlorofluorocarbons,

00:50:22.570 --> 00:50:25.292
put them on Mars at part
per million levels, which

00:50:25.292 --> 00:50:27.250
is a little bit more than
we're doing on Earth,

00:50:27.250 --> 00:50:28.760
but it's in the right direction.

00:50:28.760 --> 00:50:31.840
We could imagine bringing Mars--
warming Mars up, bringing it

00:50:31.840 --> 00:50:34.330
back to the thick CO2
life-supporting atmosphere it

00:50:34.330 --> 00:50:38.800
once had, introducing life from
Earth and going from there.

00:50:38.800 --> 00:50:41.650
Mars is the only planet that
this is really feasible on,

00:50:41.650 --> 00:50:43.330
I think.

00:50:43.330 --> 00:50:46.300
Simple back of the envelope
calculations like shown here.

00:50:46.300 --> 00:50:47.610
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:47.610 --> 00:50:49.810
Applied to any of the
other planets like Venus,

00:50:49.810 --> 00:50:51.170
just-- it's just ridiculous.

00:50:51.170 --> 00:50:53.500
This is what you have
to do to spin Venus up,

00:50:53.500 --> 00:50:54.850
if you are so inclined.

00:50:54.850 --> 00:50:56.050
And it's just ridiculous.

00:50:56.050 --> 00:50:59.710
Mars, for one reason or another,
is very close to the conditions

00:50:59.710 --> 00:51:01.210
one would like, and
we have evidence

00:51:01.210 --> 00:51:04.300
that it once had water,
maybe a biosphere.

00:51:04.300 --> 00:51:07.490
That could be a
blueprint for a future.

00:51:07.490 --> 00:51:09.908
Let me stop there on this
very speculative end,

00:51:09.908 --> 00:51:11.950
not that the rest of the
talk wasn't speculative,

00:51:11.950 --> 00:51:13.120
but by comparison.

00:51:13.120 --> 00:51:15.670
[CHUCKLES] And maybe we have
time for a few questions.

00:51:15.670 --> 00:51:19.044
[APPLAUSE]

00:51:23.112 --> 00:51:24.570
MODERATOR: Questions
for Dr. McKay?

00:51:27.880 --> 00:51:31.200
[INAUDIBLE] [? Professor ?]
[? Crowley? ?]

00:51:31.200 --> 00:51:34.660
AUDIENCE: I want to
push on you, personally,

00:51:34.660 --> 00:51:37.440
the most speculative
comment about sending humans

00:51:37.440 --> 00:51:40.285
in headfirst arouses
a lot of enthusiasm

00:51:40.285 --> 00:51:44.640
and might even arouse some
congressional support.

00:51:44.640 --> 00:51:46.920
I would never
[? attribute ?] [INAUDIBLE]

00:51:46.920 --> 00:51:48.318
[LAUGHTER]

00:51:49.720 --> 00:51:51.110
I would make two comments.

00:51:51.110 --> 00:51:55.020
One is, do you really believe
that considering the fraction

00:51:55.020 --> 00:51:58.070
of the GDP which we have
to collect from the coffers

00:51:58.070 --> 00:52:01.620
to [? conduct ?] such an issue
compared to how many unmanned

00:52:01.620 --> 00:52:06.079
missions, unpiloted missions,
you could send for the same

00:52:06.079 --> 00:52:10.940
amount of money, A. And B,
you would strengthen the case

00:52:10.940 --> 00:52:13.920
for this if you [? brought me ?]
the evidence that [? Harris ?]

00:52:13.920 --> 00:52:18.153
[? and Schmidtt ?] brought back
significantly better examples

00:52:18.153 --> 00:52:22.711
of lunar material
than the other--

00:52:22.711 --> 00:52:26.122
MCKAY: 60 or whatever,
[INAUDIBLE] [? astronauts? ?]

00:52:26.122 --> 00:52:27.570
AUDIENCE: Nine people?

00:52:27.570 --> 00:52:28.070
MCKAY: Yeah.

00:52:28.070 --> 00:52:30.070
AUDIENCE: Or 11
people who [INAUDIBLE]

00:52:30.070 --> 00:52:31.902
the surface, because
in fact, he was

00:52:31.902 --> 00:52:35.520
the human who had all that
processes [INAUDIBLE]..

00:52:35.520 --> 00:52:36.410
MCKAY: Right, right.

00:52:36.410 --> 00:52:39.282
Well, let me answer the second
question first, especially

00:52:39.282 --> 00:52:40.740
since I've forgotten
the first one.

00:52:40.740 --> 00:52:41.782
You'll have to remind me.

00:52:41.782 --> 00:52:42.600
[LAUGHTER]

00:52:42.600 --> 00:52:45.210
Which is that he was
only looking for rocks.

00:52:45.210 --> 00:52:46.800
We're searching for
fossils and life,

00:52:46.800 --> 00:52:49.660
and the task is a
little more complex.

00:52:49.660 --> 00:52:52.290
So I'm not sure it's
a fair comparison.

00:52:52.290 --> 00:52:55.050
But the first question
was, does it really

00:52:55.050 --> 00:52:57.150
make sense to involve humans?

00:52:57.150 --> 00:53:00.460
And I think you can
answer that in two ways.

00:53:00.460 --> 00:53:04.110
One way is that humans
do bring, I think,

00:53:04.110 --> 00:53:07.480
capabilities that are useful
in this particular task.

00:53:07.480 --> 00:53:11.130
I think the other way to answer
it is that science is not

00:53:11.130 --> 00:53:14.290
the only reason we go to Mars.

00:53:14.290 --> 00:53:15.815
And I think that when we--

00:53:15.815 --> 00:53:17.440
science is one of
the things humans do,

00:53:17.440 --> 00:53:19.690
and it's certainly one of
the things we'll do on Mars.

00:53:19.690 --> 00:53:22.380
But from my point
of view, there's

00:53:22.380 --> 00:53:24.060
broader reasons
for going to Mars.

00:53:24.060 --> 00:53:27.460
And those reasons all
tie to human exploration.

00:53:27.460 --> 00:53:29.680
So I think human
exploration is--

00:53:29.680 --> 00:53:32.830
is-- I wouldn't
say is inevitable,

00:53:32.830 --> 00:53:34.320
but I think it's intrinsic.

00:53:34.320 --> 00:53:35.340
It's intrinsic.

00:53:35.340 --> 00:53:39.420
It's just part of the way we do
things, exploration, adventure,

00:53:39.420 --> 00:53:42.150
whatever you want to call it.

00:53:42.150 --> 00:53:48.520
So I resist the calculus that
says how much bits per dollar

00:53:48.520 --> 00:53:51.450
can you get with robotic mission
versus how many bits per dollar

00:53:51.450 --> 00:53:53.280
or how many papers
per dollar could you

00:53:53.280 --> 00:53:54.900
get if you send humans?

00:53:54.900 --> 00:53:57.030
I think that there's
more to life than just

00:53:57.030 --> 00:53:58.555
papers and scientific journals.

00:53:58.555 --> 00:54:00.930
I don't know what it is yet,
but I'm sure it's out there.

00:54:00.930 --> 00:54:02.320
[LAUGHS]

00:54:02.320 --> 00:54:05.737
And so I-- and I think that that
argument is an argument that

00:54:05.737 --> 00:54:06.651
will--

00:54:06.651 --> 00:54:09.990
that I think has some merit
outside scientific circles,

00:54:09.990 --> 00:54:12.000
with Congress and with
the public, that there's

00:54:12.000 --> 00:54:16.250
more to the reasons we go
to Mars than just science.

00:54:16.250 --> 00:54:17.767
And I-- to touch
on part of them,

00:54:17.767 --> 00:54:19.350
is in some sense
what I'm saying here.

00:54:19.350 --> 00:54:20.500
Does Mars have a future?

00:54:20.500 --> 00:54:22.800
I think one of the key
questions about Mars is,

00:54:22.800 --> 00:54:26.730
is it potentially a
site for human activity?

00:54:26.730 --> 00:54:28.290
Is it potentially
a planet that we

00:54:28.290 --> 00:54:31.500
could imagine a large
human presence on,

00:54:31.500 --> 00:54:32.670
at some point in the future?

00:54:32.670 --> 00:54:35.190
That is not just a
scientific question.

00:54:35.190 --> 00:54:36.890
That is a question
that deals with,

00:54:36.890 --> 00:54:39.780
what do we imagine our role
as a civilization or our role

00:54:39.780 --> 00:54:41.620
as beings?

00:54:41.620 --> 00:54:44.820
And so I realize it's
kind of a mushy answer,

00:54:44.820 --> 00:54:46.380
but that's the best I can do.

00:54:46.380 --> 00:54:51.090
I can't-- if one were
decide that only science was

00:54:51.090 --> 00:54:53.520
the criteria and that was
all that we were interested

00:54:53.520 --> 00:54:56.640
in, then I would think that
certainly we could do much

00:54:56.640 --> 00:54:59.730
better, much more, much faster
with just robotic probes.

00:54:59.730 --> 00:55:02.702
And we could riddle the planet
with sample return holes

00:55:02.702 --> 00:55:04.410
and bring them back
for analysis on Earth

00:55:04.410 --> 00:55:06.660
for the cost of a
human exploration.

00:55:06.660 --> 00:55:08.005
But that's not the calculus.

00:55:08.005 --> 00:55:08.922
MODERATOR: [INAUDIBLE]

00:55:08.922 --> 00:55:11.520
AUDIENCE: I understand that
Viking did not find any

00:55:11.520 --> 00:55:14.280
evidence for organics,
but is the upper limit

00:55:14.280 --> 00:55:16.280
for the [? organics ?]
[? at the ?] [? Viking ?]

00:55:16.280 --> 00:55:18.447
[? site ?] consistent with
the Allan Hill meteroite?

00:55:18.447 --> 00:55:20.238
In other words, if you
took the meteorite--

00:55:20.238 --> 00:55:20.800
MCKAY: Right.

00:55:20.800 --> 00:55:22.215
And commutated it into dust.

00:55:22.215 --> 00:55:22.760
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:55:22.760 --> 00:55:23.060
MCKAY: Yeah.

00:55:23.060 --> 00:55:24.380
AUDIENCE: Distributed
it [? around-- ?]

00:55:24.380 --> 00:55:24.650
MCKAY: Right.

00:55:24.650 --> 00:55:25.042
AUDIENCE:
[? Would the Viking have ?]

00:55:25.042 --> 00:55:26.220
[? found the ?] [? evidence ?]
[? for organics? ?]

00:55:26.220 --> 00:55:27.750
MCKAY: Yeah, that's
a good question.

00:55:27.750 --> 00:55:29.940
The limits-- I know
the limits on Viking.

00:55:29.940 --> 00:55:32.850
I don't know what the equivalent
concentration in meteorites

00:55:32.850 --> 00:55:34.200
are, but it's pretty low.

00:55:34.200 --> 00:55:36.480
The limits on Viking
were parts per billion

00:55:36.480 --> 00:55:39.450
in the heavy organics
in the soil material.

00:55:39.450 --> 00:55:41.550
I believe that the
concentration of the meteorite

00:55:41.550 --> 00:55:46.710
is higher than that, but
I'm not 100% sure of that.

00:55:46.710 --> 00:55:50.650
I know that if you just look at
the meteoric in-fall on Mars,

00:55:50.650 --> 00:55:52.650
the rate at which organics
should be coming down

00:55:52.650 --> 00:55:55.620
from just stuff falling
onto the surface of Mars,

00:55:55.620 --> 00:55:58.507
then you would have expected
Viking to have seen something.

00:55:58.507 --> 00:56:00.090
And so there must
be some agent that's

00:56:00.090 --> 00:56:03.583
actively destroying organics
on the surface of Mars.

00:56:03.583 --> 00:56:05.250
Not based on the Allan
Hills meteorites,

00:56:05.250 --> 00:56:07.260
but based on meteorites
falling on Mars,

00:56:07.260 --> 00:56:10.620
[? carbonations, ?] meteorites,
or whatever falling on Mars.

00:56:10.620 --> 00:56:12.870
Doesn't have to be a
very powerful agent,

00:56:12.870 --> 00:56:16.082
but it has to be an agent
there destroying organics.

00:56:16.082 --> 00:56:17.540
MODERATOR: Professor
[? Caravan? ?]

00:56:17.540 --> 00:56:18.165
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:56:18.165 --> 00:56:23.712
I can understand the scientific
scenario of understanding Mars,

00:56:23.712 --> 00:56:27.183
but this business of
re-engineering it is a little

00:56:27.183 --> 00:56:29.516
different, [? don't you ?]
[? think? ?] The question is,

00:56:29.516 --> 00:56:32.207
what's the time scale for that,
and is there any chance that

00:56:32.207 --> 00:56:34.290
humans would be around
long enough to pull it off?

00:56:34.290 --> 00:56:36.550
MCKAY: Well, that's
a good question.

00:56:36.550 --> 00:56:39.750
And I might be able to find
a chart here that answers it,

00:56:39.750 --> 00:56:42.840
in that it's hard to
understand, to predict,

00:56:42.840 --> 00:56:44.970
what the scale would be.

00:56:44.970 --> 00:56:49.710
But one can do a simple
calculation in which--

00:56:49.710 --> 00:56:53.850
just look at the initial
and final states,

00:56:53.850 --> 00:56:56.387
sort of a delta H change
in [INAUDIBLE] required

00:56:56.387 --> 00:56:57.970
to go from an initial
and final state,

00:56:57.970 --> 00:57:02.700
and then calculate what's the
only logical energy source,

00:57:02.700 --> 00:57:04.540
which is sunlight.

00:57:04.540 --> 00:57:05.745
So I did this calculation.

00:57:05.745 --> 00:57:07.620
Just take an initial
state and a final state,

00:57:07.620 --> 00:57:10.920
sort of cold and
dry, warm and wet,

00:57:10.920 --> 00:57:15.180
and calculate the mass involved
in the change in energy,

00:57:15.180 --> 00:57:16.860
the delta H, and
then just divide

00:57:16.860 --> 00:57:19.128
that by the solar
constant on Mars,

00:57:19.128 --> 00:57:21.420
because that's the only
realistic energy source that we

00:57:21.420 --> 00:57:22.230
have available to harness.

00:57:22.230 --> 00:57:23.160
And so that number--

00:57:23.160 --> 00:57:25.770
if that number is 10
to the 8, well, go home

00:57:25.770 --> 00:57:27.430
and think of something else.

00:57:27.430 --> 00:57:29.430
But that-- no, those
numbers aren't 10 to the 8.

00:57:29.430 --> 00:57:32.340
Those numbers are
1, of order 1, which

00:57:32.340 --> 00:57:33.870
means that with
perfect efficiency,

00:57:33.870 --> 00:57:35.790
you could use every
single photon,

00:57:35.790 --> 00:57:37.787
you could undergo these
changes in a year.

00:57:37.787 --> 00:57:39.870
Well, obviously you can't
have perfect efficiency,

00:57:39.870 --> 00:57:42.360
but the efficiency for,
say, greenhouse effects

00:57:42.360 --> 00:57:43.562
can be quite high.

00:57:43.562 --> 00:57:45.270
They can be on the
order of a few percent

00:57:45.270 --> 00:57:48.150
if we were to introduce parts
per million of these gases

00:57:48.150 --> 00:57:48.660
on Mars.

00:57:48.660 --> 00:57:50.610
They could-- efficiency for
trapping solar energy could

00:57:50.610 --> 00:57:52.235
be on the order of
a few percent, which

00:57:52.235 --> 00:57:54.900
means that on timescales
of hundred years or so,

00:57:54.900 --> 00:57:59.040
Mars could be warm and quite
pleasant compared to today.

00:57:59.040 --> 00:58:01.930
Wouldn't be breathable,
but it would be warm.

00:58:01.930 --> 00:58:04.740
The one place where this
calculation doesn't auger well

00:58:04.740 --> 00:58:07.620
is down here, when you
talk about making oxygen.

00:58:07.620 --> 00:58:08.813
Then the scale is 17 years.

00:58:08.813 --> 00:58:10.230
But if you look
at the only way we

00:58:10.230 --> 00:58:12.190
know how to do that
on a global scale,

00:58:12.190 --> 00:58:14.965
is with self-replicating
machines called plants,

00:58:14.965 --> 00:58:17.340
their efficiency on Earth,
which presumably is maximized,

00:58:17.340 --> 00:58:19.798
because they've been working
on this for millions of years,

00:58:19.798 --> 00:58:21.480
is 10 to the minus
4, then you get

00:58:21.480 --> 00:58:23.780
a number that's way out
there, a time of 5 years.

00:58:23.780 --> 00:58:25.530
So this is the only
way I can answer that.

00:58:25.530 --> 00:58:26.490
It's not a prediction.

00:58:26.490 --> 00:58:30.322
It's just an energy
efficiency calculation.

00:58:30.322 --> 00:58:31.770
MODERATOR: Yes?

00:58:31.770 --> 00:58:35.630
AUDIENCE: Let's assume that we
would be able to manage to get

00:58:35.630 --> 00:58:39.040
an atmosphere that's somewhat
thicker and has a CO2 content

00:58:39.040 --> 00:58:42.100
like [INAUDIBLE],, maybe even
by using some of the CO2

00:58:42.100 --> 00:58:42.959
that's [INAUDIBLE]--

00:58:42.959 --> 00:58:43.626
MCKAY: Absorbed.

00:58:43.626 --> 00:58:44.751
AUDIENCE: --into the rocks.

00:58:44.751 --> 00:58:47.173
Wouldn't the same thing
happen that happened--

00:58:47.173 --> 00:58:47.673
MCKAY: Yep.

00:58:47.673 --> 00:58:48.126
AUDIENCE: --10
billion years ago,

00:58:48.126 --> 00:58:50.183
because the basic
cycles are not in place?

00:58:50.183 --> 00:58:50.850
MCKAY: Yep, yep.

00:58:50.850 --> 00:58:52.065
We're not going to
start plate tectonics--

00:58:52.065 --> 00:58:52.380
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:58:52.380 --> 00:58:52.890
MCKAY: --on Mars.

00:58:52.890 --> 00:58:53.500
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] lost--

00:58:53.500 --> 00:58:53.930
MCKAY: Yep.

00:58:53.930 --> 00:58:54.430
Yeah.

00:58:54.430 --> 00:58:57.150
Eventually it'll go back,
and our estimate for how long

00:58:57.150 --> 00:58:59.517
that took is about 10
to the 8 years, 10 to 7,

00:58:59.517 --> 00:59:00.347
10 to the 8 years.

00:59:00.347 --> 00:59:02.430
So what I view it is you're
spreading the mortgage

00:59:02.430 --> 00:59:04.700
payments out over 10 to
the 7, 10 to the 8 year.

00:59:04.700 --> 00:59:07.410
Now, that's short compared
to the lifetime of a planet,

00:59:07.410 --> 00:59:10.220
but it's long compared to
congressional funding cycles--

00:59:10.220 --> 00:59:10.720
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:10.720 --> 00:59:12.100
--or even lifetimes.

00:59:12.100 --> 00:59:14.560
It's even long compared
to a civilization.

00:59:14.560 --> 00:59:16.950
So I think that-- and
maybe 10 to the 8 years,

00:59:16.950 --> 00:59:19.900
somebody else will have a
good idea for what to do next.

00:59:19.900 --> 00:59:21.390
So I think it's--

00:59:21.390 --> 00:59:23.580
yeah, we don't solve the
problem for all time,

00:59:23.580 --> 00:59:25.220
but nothing is
solved for all time.

00:59:25.220 --> 00:59:27.420
Even Earth is not
habitable for all time.

00:59:27.420 --> 00:59:29.550
I mean, we're a
middle-aged planet.

00:59:29.550 --> 00:59:31.740
At best we've got another--

00:59:31.740 --> 00:59:34.230
as long to live as we've lived,
maybe even less, depending

00:59:34.230 --> 00:59:36.690
on your belief in
those scenarios for CO2

00:59:36.690 --> 00:59:38.050
and solar luminosity.

00:59:38.050 --> 00:59:41.037
So nothing lasts
forever, despite what

00:59:41.037 --> 00:59:42.120
you've heard on the radio.

00:59:42.120 --> 00:59:42.553
[CHUCKLES]

00:59:42.553 --> 00:59:43.420
MODERATOR: [INAUDIBLE]

00:59:43.420 --> 00:59:44.837
AUDIENCE: You said
it was unlikely

00:59:44.837 --> 00:59:46.640
that we would find
subsurface life on Mars.

00:59:46.640 --> 00:59:48.265
Could you say a little
bit more about--

00:59:48.265 --> 00:59:50.420
MCKAY: Well, I
think it's unlikely,

00:59:50.420 --> 00:59:51.980
and I base-- this
is a gut feeling.

00:59:51.980 --> 00:59:53.660
We have no data that
argues against it.

00:59:53.660 --> 00:59:56.780
I base it on two to
intuitive senses.

00:59:56.780 --> 00:59:58.880
One, if we look at Mars,
we don't see any evidence

00:59:58.880 --> 01:00:00.200
of recent activity.

01:00:00.200 --> 01:00:02.660
Granted, the SNC meteorites
indicate volcanism

01:00:02.660 --> 01:00:04.230
200 million years ago.

01:00:04.230 --> 01:00:06.187
That's yesterday,
geologically speaking.

01:00:06.187 --> 01:00:08.270
Maybe there's subsurface
heat, but subsurface heat

01:00:08.270 --> 01:00:09.070
isn't enough.

01:00:09.070 --> 01:00:11.750
We need subsurface heat
and subsurface groundwater,

01:00:11.750 --> 01:00:13.670
and we need chemical production.

01:00:13.670 --> 01:00:16.160
It just looks like there's
too many ifs, geologically.

01:00:16.160 --> 01:00:19.310
Biologically, it means we have
to postulate a biosphere that

01:00:19.310 --> 01:00:22.220
can sustain itself, that can
solve recruitment and dispersal

01:00:22.220 --> 01:00:22.760
problems.

01:00:22.760 --> 01:00:24.890
You know, one geothermal
site goes off.

01:00:24.890 --> 01:00:27.260
How is it going to
get to the other one?

01:00:27.260 --> 01:00:28.430
That sort of issues.

01:00:28.430 --> 01:00:31.610
Over billions of
years, it just--

01:00:31.610 --> 01:00:33.350
my sense of it is no.

01:00:33.350 --> 01:00:36.530
Now, people with all the same
data say that their sense of it

01:00:36.530 --> 01:00:37.920
is yes, it's worth looking for.

01:00:37.920 --> 01:00:41.440
So that's just the what-- just
the differences of opinion.

01:00:41.440 --> 01:00:43.147
AUDIENCE: Does that
mean, then, that you

01:00:43.147 --> 01:00:46.021
feel that the forward
decontamination problem is not

01:00:46.021 --> 01:00:46.740
an issue?

01:00:46.740 --> 01:00:49.135
Unless [? some people are trying
 to ?] load up a spacecraft with

01:00:49.135 --> 01:00:49.620
[? fossils? ?]

01:00:49.620 --> 01:00:51.537
MCKAY: Well, the forward
contamination problem

01:00:51.537 --> 01:00:54.810
is a big issue, because
if we go to Mars

01:00:54.810 --> 01:00:57.290
we might see a signal
that's biological, that's

01:00:57.290 --> 01:00:59.090
due to something
we carried with us,

01:00:59.090 --> 01:01:02.600
which would cause a big
problem in future missions.

01:01:02.600 --> 01:01:04.370
So suppose we went
to Mars with an oven

01:01:04.370 --> 01:01:08.030
to analyze volatiles that took
a sample and heated it up,

01:01:08.030 --> 01:01:09.710
and that sample was--

01:01:09.710 --> 01:01:11.090
that oven was contaminated.

01:01:11.090 --> 01:01:14.900
And we saw a signature of
reduced gases coming out.

01:01:14.900 --> 01:01:18.050
And that would be a disaster,
because that would then--

01:01:18.050 --> 01:01:18.635
masses--

01:01:18.635 --> 01:01:20.510
bureaucratic machinery
would kick in and say,

01:01:20.510 --> 01:01:21.440
evidence for life.

01:01:21.440 --> 01:01:23.210
Everybody stop.

01:01:23.210 --> 01:01:25.220
We've got to worry about
this, when it's really

01:01:25.220 --> 01:01:26.470
just contamination from Earth.

01:01:26.470 --> 01:01:29.180
So I think that the forward
contamination problem is mostly

01:01:29.180 --> 01:01:30.140
to--

01:01:30.140 --> 01:01:32.850
it's mostly just doing
things properly and cleanly.

01:01:32.850 --> 01:01:35.150
It's the same thing in a
lab, aseptic techniques,

01:01:35.150 --> 01:01:38.900
so you don't culture yourself
or your E. coli in terms

01:01:38.900 --> 01:01:40.740
of your experimental results.

01:01:40.740 --> 01:01:42.920
Now, I'm advocating
forward contamination

01:01:42.920 --> 01:01:46.050
on purpose, later on, where you
don't accidentally bring gunk

01:01:46.050 --> 01:01:46.550
with you.

01:01:46.550 --> 01:01:49.070
You purposely bring
gunk with you.

01:01:49.070 --> 01:01:50.720
So it's a different
point of view.

01:01:50.720 --> 01:01:54.193
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] much
harder on a manned mission.

01:01:54.193 --> 01:01:56.610
MCKAY: Controlling it is much
harder on human exploration,

01:01:56.610 --> 01:01:57.610
but it's not impossible.

01:01:57.610 --> 01:02:00.477
We do biological collection with
aseptic technique all the time.

01:02:00.477 --> 01:02:02.810
Biologists have been doing
it for-- field biologists has

01:02:02.810 --> 01:02:05.192
been doing it for centuries.

01:02:05.192 --> 01:02:06.650
It doesn't take a
rocket scientist.

01:02:06.650 --> 01:02:07.525
It takes a biologist.

01:02:07.525 --> 01:02:08.628
[LAUGHTER]

01:02:08.628 --> 01:02:09.420
MODERATOR: Melissa?

01:02:09.420 --> 01:02:11.420
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
What do you think--

01:02:11.420 --> 01:02:13.920
have you thought about
any ethical reasons behind

01:02:13.920 --> 01:02:17.795
[INAUDIBLE] behind transforming
another planet when [INAUDIBLE]

01:02:17.795 --> 01:02:19.420
[? trying ?]
[? to fix ?] [INAUDIBLE]..

01:02:19.420 --> 01:02:19.962
MCKAY: Right.

01:02:19.962 --> 01:02:21.040
No, that's a good point.

01:02:21.040 --> 01:02:22.120
There's two points there.

01:02:22.120 --> 01:02:24.010
One is just as--

01:02:24.010 --> 01:02:26.440
expenses in space versus
expenses on Earth.

01:02:26.440 --> 01:02:29.463
And I would apply that logic to
the whole federal budget, not

01:02:29.463 --> 01:02:31.880
just the space program, which
is a trivial fraction of it.

01:02:31.880 --> 01:02:34.380
There's a lot of things which
I think we shouldn't be buying

01:02:34.380 --> 01:02:36.430
and should be building
better schools instead.

01:02:36.430 --> 01:02:37.580
That's one issue.

01:02:37.580 --> 01:02:40.950
The other issue is, do we
really want to go to a planet

01:02:40.950 --> 01:02:43.750
and change its natural
course of affairs?

01:02:43.750 --> 01:02:46.240
And I've only been answering
the question of how.

01:02:46.240 --> 01:02:47.957
I'm just doing numbers here.

01:02:47.957 --> 01:02:49.540
Now we come to the
question of should?

01:02:49.540 --> 01:02:50.150
Should we?

01:02:50.150 --> 01:02:51.862
Is this something we'd
really want to do?

01:02:51.862 --> 01:02:53.320
And it really boils
down to what do

01:02:53.320 --> 01:02:55.870
we think is important
in the environment?

01:02:55.870 --> 01:02:57.970
The environment,
per se, or the fact

01:02:57.970 --> 01:02:59.680
that the environment
has life in it?

01:02:59.680 --> 01:03:01.060
On Earth, there's
no distinction.

01:03:01.060 --> 01:03:03.610
It's the generate case, because
the environment on Earth

01:03:03.610 --> 01:03:05.050
is biological.

01:03:05.050 --> 01:03:07.240
Every corner of the
Earth is connected

01:03:07.240 --> 01:03:09.010
to some biological component.

01:03:09.010 --> 01:03:10.750
On Mars, that--
there's a splitting

01:03:10.750 --> 01:03:12.860
in that [? the generative ?]
is not the same.

01:03:12.860 --> 01:03:14.590
There's a distinct
difference between a--

01:03:14.590 --> 01:03:17.930
between a biosphere and
an environment on Mars.

01:03:17.930 --> 01:03:20.450
So now I'm speaking just
my own personal opinion.

01:03:20.450 --> 01:03:21.610
I vote for life.

01:03:21.610 --> 01:03:23.482
I'm a admitted, now,
life chauvinist.

01:03:23.482 --> 01:03:24.940
I think life's a
great thing, and I

01:03:24.940 --> 01:03:26.590
think if we could spread
life beyond Earth, that

01:03:26.590 --> 01:03:27.760
would be a good thing to do.

01:03:27.760 --> 01:03:30.220
Just a personal opinion.

01:03:30.220 --> 01:03:33.295
It's not official
NASA policy yet.

01:03:33.295 --> 01:03:34.570
But I'm working on it.

01:03:34.570 --> 01:03:36.010
[LAUGHTER]

01:03:36.010 --> 01:03:41.768
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] change
Mars before we fix Earth, or--

01:03:41.768 --> 01:03:43.310
MCKAY: I don't think
it's a compare--

01:03:43.310 --> 01:03:44.650
I don't think it's either or.

01:03:44.650 --> 01:03:47.080
I think what we might
learn about Mars,

01:03:47.080 --> 01:03:49.180
information will be relevant
to maintaining Earth.

01:03:49.180 --> 01:03:51.670
I think we have assumed
as a species management

01:03:51.670 --> 01:03:54.290
responsibilities for Earth,
whether we like it or not.

01:03:54.290 --> 01:03:56.650
And so we have to take a
course in planet management,

01:03:56.650 --> 01:03:59.050
and I think most of that course
is going to be studying Earth,

01:03:59.050 --> 01:04:01.258
but occasional field trips
to other planets, I think,

01:04:01.258 --> 01:04:02.300
are in order.

01:04:02.300 --> 01:04:03.800
And I think Mars
will tell us a lot.

01:04:03.800 --> 01:04:06.400
And if we try to build
a biosphere on Mars,

01:04:06.400 --> 01:04:08.560
I think that lesson
will be very useful.

01:04:08.560 --> 01:04:11.260
I remember years ago building
a motorcycle from parts,

01:04:11.260 --> 01:04:14.615
and that was a profound lesson
in how that motorcycle worked.

01:04:14.615 --> 01:04:16.240
It didn't work when
I tried to start it

01:04:16.240 --> 01:04:17.448
after I built it, by the way.

01:04:17.448 --> 01:04:19.910
[CHUCKLES] Which
is another lesson.

01:04:19.910 --> 01:04:22.510
But I think studying Mars
will be part of learning

01:04:22.510 --> 01:04:23.400
to manage the Earth.

01:04:23.400 --> 01:04:24.400
It's not an alternative.

01:04:24.400 --> 01:04:26.110
I don't think it's--

01:04:26.110 --> 01:04:28.990
that they're in that
relationship at all.

01:04:28.990 --> 01:04:31.334
We just don't have any
choice to manage the Earth.

01:04:31.334 --> 01:04:32.834
AUDIENCE: Chris,
one of the missions

01:04:32.834 --> 01:04:35.813
that you didn't talk about
was the 1998 [INAUDIBLE]

01:04:35.813 --> 01:04:36.355
MCKAY: Polar.

01:04:36.355 --> 01:04:39.090
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] land in
the southern layer terrain.

01:04:39.090 --> 01:04:40.248
You said, high--

01:04:40.248 --> 01:04:40.790
MCKAY: Right.

01:04:40.790 --> 01:04:43.214
AUDIENCE: --latitude and it's
go gas and chemical analyzers

01:04:43.214 --> 01:04:43.714
on it.

01:04:43.714 --> 01:04:46.693
And are the sensitivities
of that instrumentation

01:04:46.693 --> 01:04:49.675
germane to making the
kind of measurements

01:04:49.675 --> 01:04:53.723
that you talked about for
[? the pole ?] [INAUDIBLE]??

01:04:53.723 --> 01:04:55.390
MCKAY: That's an
interesting instrument.

01:04:55.390 --> 01:04:58.180
The [? TIGA ?] instrument
on Mars '98 will land.

01:04:58.180 --> 01:05:01.307
It's about 70 degrees
south, in the polar terrain.

01:05:01.307 --> 01:05:02.890
The polar terrain
isn't the best place

01:05:02.890 --> 01:05:04.480
to search for
evidence of past life

01:05:04.480 --> 01:05:06.350
because it's relatively young.

01:05:06.350 --> 01:05:08.080
So it's not in the--

01:05:08.080 --> 01:05:10.205
it's not-- my understanding
of the material they'll

01:05:10.205 --> 01:05:12.330
sample is that they're
probably seeing stuff that's

01:05:12.330 --> 01:05:14.510
on the order of 10 to the
6, 10 to the 7 years old,

01:05:14.510 --> 01:05:16.930
so it's not really going
back into the ancient stuff.

01:05:16.930 --> 01:05:19.045
We'd really want to
drill down, I think,

01:05:19.045 --> 01:05:20.920
deep enough that we're
below the annual wave,

01:05:20.920 --> 01:05:22.378
and probably deep
enough that we're

01:05:22.378 --> 01:05:26.050
below the obliquity wave,
so changes in obliquity.

01:05:26.050 --> 01:05:28.630
So really, deep down, to
get old stuff that's been

01:05:28.630 --> 01:05:31.158
frozen for billions of years.

01:05:31.158 --> 01:05:33.200
But the [? TIGA ?] instrument
may find something,

01:05:33.200 --> 01:05:35.980
and it's an example where
forward contamination matters.

01:05:35.980 --> 01:05:39.043
If it sees [? and it's ?]
evolved gases, methane,

01:05:39.043 --> 01:05:41.210
and that turns out to be
something that they cooked,

01:05:41.210 --> 01:05:43.660
that they brought with them, a
piece of [? Billboy's ?] Pizza

01:05:43.660 --> 01:05:45.440
or something, you know, that's
going to be a big problem.

01:05:45.440 --> 01:05:47.950
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] thinking
about the [? sterilization. ?]

01:05:47.950 --> 01:05:50.075
MCKAY: Well, sterilization
isn't required, I think.

01:05:50.075 --> 01:05:52.245
It's just clean, cleanliness.

01:05:52.245 --> 01:05:53.120
Clean room technique.

01:05:53.120 --> 01:05:55.060
I think that's the
protocols on '98

01:05:55.060 --> 01:06:00.070
are adequate for
forward contamination.

01:06:00.070 --> 01:06:03.665
MODERATOR: Well, Chris, before
I let you go and give you

01:06:03.665 --> 01:06:05.540
a [? small ?]
[? presentation, ?] let me just

01:06:05.540 --> 01:06:06.800
make one announcement.

01:06:06.800 --> 01:06:09.470
For those members of my
undergraduate seminar

01:06:09.470 --> 01:06:12.960
who were here, would you please
move down a bit to the front?

01:06:12.960 --> 01:06:15.700
We'll have a brief informal
discussion with Professor McKay

01:06:15.700 --> 01:06:18.580
and I'll let you
sign in for the day.

01:06:18.580 --> 01:06:19.330
MCKAY: And a quiz.

01:06:19.330 --> 01:06:20.060
MODERATOR: I told
the rest of you--

01:06:20.060 --> 01:06:22.580
I told you an hour ago you're
in for an exciting time.

01:06:22.580 --> 01:06:27.340
I underestimated even what
Chris would produce for us.

01:06:27.340 --> 01:06:30.260
This certificate-- it says
certificate of appreciation.

01:06:30.260 --> 01:06:31.440
That's an understatement.

01:06:31.440 --> 01:06:32.700
Chris, you did a
wonderful job, and we're--

01:06:32.700 --> 01:06:32.920
MCKAY: Thanks, Larry.

01:06:32.920 --> 01:06:33.860
MODERATOR: --delighted
[INAUDIBLE]..

01:06:33.860 --> 01:06:34.652
MCKAY: My pleasure.

01:06:34.652 --> 01:06:37.210
[APPLAUSE]

